We recall the following concept. 

\begin{definition}
A point $x'$ is the {\bf first return} of a point $x$ to a transverse $T$ along a leaf arc $C \colon [a,b] \to \F(x)$ if the restriction $C|_{(a,b)}$ is a simple curve in $\F(x) \setminus T$ with $C(a) = x \in T$ and $C(b) = x' \in T$.  
For such first returns along $C$, the existence of fibered charts implies a first return map $T' \to T''$ along $C$ between sub-arcs $T'$ and $T''$ of the transverse $T$, called a {\bf first return map} {\rm(}or holonomy map{\rm)} along $C$ to $T$. 
