To demonstrate Theorem~\ref{non-existence_structural_stability}, we show several lemmas. 

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:no_sing_leaf_int-}
Fix any $p \in \R_{\geq 1} \sqcup \{ \infty \}$ and let $M$ be a surface. 
Suppose that $\mathrm{vol}_g(M) < \infty$ if $p \in \R_{\geq 1}$. 
%For any $r \in \Z_{>0}$, a
Then any singular foliation on $M$ whose union of singular leaves has a non-empty interior is not structurally stable in $\mathcal{F}^q(M)$ with respect to the topology $\mathcal{O}^p_{w,\F^q(M)}$. 
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Let $\mathcal{G}$ be a singular foliation on a surface $M$ whose union of singular leaves has a non-empty interior. 
Put $S := \mathrm{int}(\mathop{\mathrm{Sing}}(\mathcal{G})) \neq \emptyset$. 
Then, any leaves of the restriction of $\mathcal{G}$ to the surface $S$ are singletons. 
%
Fix any distance weight $w \colon [0,\infty] \to [0,1]$ and any positive number $\varepsilon >0$. 
By Lemma~\ref{lem:singular_pt_nbd02}, there is a positive number $\delta > 0$ satisfying the following inequality for any singular foliation $\F$ on $M$:
\[ 
\sup_{x \in B(\mathop{\mathrm{Sing}}(\F),\delta)} d_H(X_{w,\F}(x), X_{w,\G}(x)) < \varepsilon
\]
%\[
%\dfrac{\mathrm{vol}_g(B(\mathop{\mathrm{Sing}}(\F),\delta))}{\mathrm{vol}_g(M)} < 1
%\]
Replacing the singular foliation $\mathcal{G}\vert_S$ into new one by using a bump function, we can choose a singular foliation $\F \in \mathcal{F}^q(M)$ with $X_\F \vert_{M -S} = X_\mathcal{G}\vert_{M -S}$, $\mathrm{int}(\mathop{\mathrm{Sing}}(\mathcal{\F})) = \emptyset$, and $S \subseteq B(\mathop{\mathrm{Sing}}(\F),\delta)$.
Then $\partial S = \partial \mathop{\mathrm{Sing}}(\G) \subseteq \mathop{\mathrm{Sing}}(\F)$. 
Therefore, we have the following inequalities: 
\[
\begin{split}
D^\infty_{w}(\F, \mathcal{G}) &= \sup_{x \in M} d_H(X_{w,\F}(x), X_{w,\G}(x))\\
&= \sup_{x \in B(\mathop{\mathrm{Sing}}(\F),\delta)}d_H(X_{w,\F}(x), X_{w,\G}(x)) < \varepsilon
\end{split}
