\paragraph*{\bf{Conclusion:}}
%{\color{blue}The current version of the conclusion is long and some parts have been repeated several times. Here is my suggestion: In this article, we examine the impact of finite size on phase synchronization in Kuramoto oscillators with inertia and 2-simplex coupling. While analytical calculations do not indicate a synchronization transition in the thermodynamic limit, we find that finite-sized networks can lead to an abrupt synchronization jump. This transition is driven by fluctuations in the order parameter, which can overcome unstable orbits in smaller networks. As network size increases, these fluctuations decrease, requiring stronger coupling for synchronization. {\bf {We have not shown this anywhere:}} Additionally, higher inertia increases fluctuations in the synchronized state, which can hinder the transition back to incoherence, necessitating higher coupling strengths.}
%The findings suggest that inertia counteracts the effects of finite size, and synchronization transitions can occur in systems where it is not predicted in the thermodynamic limit. Future research could explore the incorporation of network architectures to better reflect real-world systems.


This article investigates the impact of finite size on the phase synchronization of Kuramoto oscillators with inertia and 2-simplex coupling. Although analytical calculations do not suggest a synchronization transition in the thermodynamic limit, we observe that finite-sized networks can experience an abrupt synchronization jump.

This synchronization transition is driven by fluctuations in the order parameter. For smaller networks, these fluctuations can be significant enough to cross the unstable orbit in the bistable region. Starting from a random initial phase condition, the system transitions from an incoherent stable state to a synchronized stable state as soon as fluctuations in $r_1$ become large enough to cross the unstable orbit.

As the size of the system increases, the fluctuations in $r_1$ decrease. Consequently, stronger coupling strengths are generally required to achieve this synchronization jump, since increasing the coupling strength brings the unstable orbit closer to the $r_1 = 0$ state. Additionally, for networks of the same size, increasing $m$ also necessitates a stronger coupling strength to effect this transition from an incoherent state to a synchronized state. This suggests that inertia acts to resist the effects associated with finite network size.
Future research could explore the incorporation of network architectures that better reflect real-world systems.

%This resistance is more pronounced in the time series analysis of $r_1$ for different inertia strengths. With an increase in inertia, the fluctuations in $r_1$ in the synchronized state increase. With an increase in $m$, one can expect that the fluctuation in $r_1$ in the coupled synchronized state will be high enough to cross the unstable orbit, bringing the system back to the incoherent state. However, with an increase in $m$, one needs a very high value of $K_{2c}$. As the unstable state asymptotically approaches $r_1=0$, fluctuations in $r_1$ in the synchronized state, even if drive the system to cross the unstable orbit landing to $r_1=0$ state, the fluctuations in $r_1$ in the incoherent state will again drive back the system to the coherent state. Hence, we might not witness a jump from the synchronized to incoherent.
