\section{Polariton number derivation} \label{sec:A2}



We estimate the polartion number at $P = 1.6P_{th}$ in the $1.2~\mu m$ trap according to the technique described in \cite{PhysRevX.5.031002}. Polariton number is given by:
\begin{equation}
    N = \frac{\alpha R \tau}{n_{tot} |C|^2}
\end{equation}
were $\alpha = 0.46 e^- sec^{-1}$ is the camera conversion rate, $R_{tr} = 87.5 \times 10^6  ~\text{counts} ~ s^{-1}$ the integrated counts for the trapped polariton condensate and $R_{pr} = 812 \times 10^6 ~\text{counts} ~ s^{-1}$ for the un-trapped condensate, $n_{tot} = T_{\text{optics}} \times   QE_{\text{camera}} =7.3 \times 10^{-6}$ is the total efficiency of the collection system: $T_{\text{optics}}$ is the efficiency of the optical setup, $QE_{\text{camera}}$ is the quantum efficiency of the camera. The polariton lifetime is $\tau = 10~ps$ and the photon fraction is $|C|^2 = 0.4$ The resulting steady state polariton numbers are:
\begin{gather*}
    N_{tr}= 150 \pm22\\
    N_{pr} = 1280 \pm 420
\end{gather*}
 Were $N_{tr}$ is the steady state number of trapped polaritons, and $N_{pr}$ is the steady state polariton count without an implanted trap, both at their respective $P = 1.6P_{th}$.

