\section{Results}
We benchmarked these gLMs and their variants across five datasets, as shown in Appendix~\ref{model_eval}. Representative models were selected for further analysis in the following sections.
\subsection{Zero-shot \ourmethod prediction performance deteriorates for sequences with low gLM likelihood}

The evolutionary plausibility of a sequence, as predicted by a gLM, strongly correlates with the gLM's performance in predicting mutation effects for the sequence. 
% To assess the ability of gLMs to make zero-shot functional predictions of DNA sequences, we first take functional expression cassettes and generate its corresponding \ourmethod. 
Figure~\ref{fig:correlation}A compares gLM prediction performance of individual functional cassettes and their predicted LL for two models, Evo-2-7B and NucleotideTransformer-2.5B-MS. Here success rate for each cassette is defined as the proportion of \ourmethod (11 in eukaryote, 19 in prokaryote) whose LL score is lower than the original, nonmutant cassette.
For both models, we observe strong correlations between the nonmutant LL score and the gLM's ability to identify LOF mutants. For example, for the E.coli (Low) dataset \cite{Lagator_data}, zero-shot \ourmethod prediction with NT-2.5B-MS is nearly impossible when the nonmutant LL is below -25, and easily accomplished for nonmutants with LL above -22. In fact, Figure~\ref{fig:correlation}B shows that all models, despite variations in architecture and training, exhibits a positive correlation between sequence likelihood and mutant prediction performance.

The Evo-2-7B model achieves the best overall performance in zero-shot \ourmethod prediction. Evaluating zero-shot prediction using all sequences within a dataset (See Methods), Figure~\ref{fig:correlation}C shows that Evo-2-7B achieves the best overall performance across all five datasets. Specifically for the E.coli and yeast MPRA data using random promoters (left), which are less evolutionarily plausible than more natural sequences shown on the right (Appendix Figure~\ref{fig:compare-ll}). 
% Certain models, such as GPN-Promoter, perform poorly on random promoter cassettes with low likelihood despite doing well on more natural promoter cassettes with higher likelihood.


