Let $r\geq 2$ be a fixed integer. Let $R=T_1\times_k \cdots\times_k T_r$, where for each $1\leq i\leq r$ we have $T_i=k[\![\chi_i]\!]$ with $\chi_i=\{X_1^{(i)},\ldots,X_{n_i}^{(i)}\}$ for an integer $n_i\geq 1$. Also, consider the ideal $\Gamma$ of $k[\![\chi_1,\ldots,\chi_r]\!]$ generated by the set
$$
\left\{\chi_i\chi_j\mid 1\leq i,j\leq r\right\}.
$$
Note that $R\cong k[\![\chi_1,\ldots,\chi_r]\!]/\Gamma$; see~\cite[3.3]{NST}. Moreover, $\m_R\cong (\overline{\chi}_1)\oplus\cdots\oplus(\overline{\chi}_r)$, where $\overline{\chi}_i$ is the set of variables $\chi_i$ modulo $\Gamma$, for each integer $1\leq i\leq r$. For simplicity we assume that these isomorphisms are equalities. Then, setting $J_i=\m_R/(\overline{\chi}_i)$, for each $1\leq i\leq r$, we have
$$
R/J_i\cong T_i
$$
which is a regular local ring. Therefore, $A^+_{R/J_i}=0$, which implies that $\mathcal{A} =0$ from Theorem~\ref{rproducts}. This gives the structure of $A_R$ using $\mathcal{W}$ introduced in Theorem~\ref{rproducts}.

