\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:introduction}

%nah, just volume and first moment of random shapes. bleh
% In the numerical simulations field, it is sometimes required to evaluate integrals over polyhedra intersected by a curved surface

In many applications of computational mechanics, e.g., the modeling of immiscible two-phase flows, it is necessary to compute the first geometric moments of a material phase contained within a polyhedral cell~\cite{Bna2015,Jones2019,Kromer2019,Kromer2023a,Tolle2022}.
The accurate estimation of these quantities is, for instance, critical to ensure mass and momentum conservation in multiphase computational fluid dynamics~\cite{Renardy2002,Dyadechko2005,Owkes2014,Owkes2017}. In practice, computing such moments consists in evaluating the intersection between a polyhedral cell---typically, a mesh element or a flux volume---and a region of $\mathbb{R}^3$ representing the material domain, often locally approximated as the region bounded by a plane or by a curved surface such as a sphere, paraboloid, or cylinder. To be used for the numerical solution of conservation laws, the estimation of these quantities is required to be highly accurate, computationally efficient, and robust.

Traditional methods for computing the moments of such clipped polyhedra include Monte-Carlo methods \cite{Evans2000, Hahn2005}, recursive octree subdivision \cite{Kudela2016, Divi2020}, or surface triangulation techniques \cite{Tolle2022}. While versatile, these approaches tend to be computationally expensive when required to reach high accuracy, making them unfit for applications that call for systematic and accurate estimates. Another way to address this problem is to use the divergence theorem so as to reduce the three-dimensional moment integrals into a collection of simpler one-dimensional integrals, for which closed-form solutions can be derived~\cite{Kromer2019, Kromer2023a, Antolin2022a}. This approach allows for greater precision, i.e., only limited by the choice of floating-point format, while keeping the computational cost low. Its downside is that it requires defining nontrivial integration domains and integrands, which are therefore limited to specific types of clipping surfaces.

Following this approach, Evrard~et al.~\cite{Evrard2023} recently derived closed-form expressions of the volume and centroid of a polyhedron clipped by a paraboloid. The resulting estimations of the first moments achieve machine precision while being several orders of magnitude faster than with other existing approaches.
The present paper aims to extend the work of Evrard~et al.~\cite{Evrard2023} to cases of polyhedra clipped by an infinite quadratic cylinder while retaining similar objectives, i.e., obtaining machine-accurate estimations of the first moments at minimal computational cost and while being robust to ambiguous and singular intersection configurations. % It follows the same methodology, and same validation procedure while adapted to this new geometry. \\

The manuscript is divided as follows: After summarizing the core methodology of~\cite{Evrard2023} in \Cref{sec:fondation}, the changes required to consider the clipping of a polyhedron by a quadratic cylinder are described in \Cref{sec:new_work}. The implementation of the new closed-form expressions in the open-source \texttt{Interface Reconstruction Library}\footnote{The source code implementing the solutions derived in this manuscript is available in the \texttt{Interface Reconstruction Library (IRL)} under Mozilla Public License 2.0 at \texttt{\url{https://github.com/cmfr-ae-illinois/interface-reconstruction-library/tree/quadratic_cutting}}.}~\cite{Chiodi2022,Evrard2023} is validated against a variety of random and hand-crafted configurations in \Cref{sec:tests}.

