\subsection{Parameter sweep for several geometries} \label{sec:paramsweep}
In this second test, we consider a selection of convex polyhedra (a regular tetrahedron, a cube, and a regular dodecahedron) and a nonconvex polyhedron (a ``hollow'' cube), constructed so that their volume is equal to unity. These polyhedra are centered at $\smash{\mathbf{x}_c = \begin{bmatrix}x_{c} & y_{c} & z_{c}\end{bmatrix}^\intercal}$ located within the cube $\smash{\begin{bmatrix}-1/2,1/2\end{bmatrix}^3}$ and rotated by $\smash{\{\theta_x, \theta_y, \theta_z\} \in [-\pi,\pi]^3}$ about the three main axes, respectively.
We then intersect each polyhedron with a cylinder defined as in \Cref{eq:defS,def:phi} with coefficients $\alpha \in [0, 6/5]$ and $\beta \in [-10, 10]$. Finally, the geometric moments of the clipped polyhedron estimated using the expressions derived in \Cref{sec:cylinder_case} are compared with those obtained through AMR.

