Because the closed-form expressions derived in \Cref{sec:cylinder_case} are only valid for polyhedra in the top halfspace, and the generalization presented in \Cref{sec:generalization} requires cutting the polyhedron in half, the program needs to consider more faces than the original polyhedron contains. Therefore, we opted not to show the timings per face as in~\cite{Evrard2023}.

As in~\cite{Evrard2023}, we recover that clipping a polyhedron by a paraboloid surface is about 6 times more expensive than clipping it by a plane. In turn, the clipping of a polyhedron by a quadratic cylinder is about 4.5 times more expensive clipping it by a paraboloid and 28 times more expensive than clipping it by a plane. This reduction in speed can be explained by the fact that the clipping by a quadratic cylinders requires making two copies of the polyhedron corresponding to its intersections with the top and bottom $z$-halfspaces, to compute new vertices and faces, and to rotate the polyhedron about the $x$-axis. These manipulations involve memory allocation and an increased amount of faces to consider, in turn increasing the unit-cost of each intersection case.

\section{Conclusions}
\label{sec:conclusions}
We derive closed-form expressions for the first moments of a polyhedron clipped by an infinite quadratic cylinder, extending the work of~\cite{Evrard2023} in which similar expressions are derived for the clipping by a paraboloid surface. While new closed-form expressions are derived, most algorithmic steps introduced in~\cite{Evrard2023} remain unchanged, enabling minimal changes to the clipping algorithm and corresponding code. The main new steps include the splitting the polyhedron by the $xy$-plane prior to clipping it by the quadratic cylinder, since the derived closed-form expressions are only valid in the $z\ge0$ halfspace. The approach is tested against several millions of randomly and hand-chosen intersection configurations. When compared with the results of \cite{Evrard2023} for the clipping by a paraboloid, our algorithm for quadratic cylinder clipping exhibits similar accuracy and robustness, for an increase in computational cost by a factor 5 or less.

