\begin{abstract}
We present the results of an experimental investigation into buoyant rigid spheres rising through concentrated collections of hydrated hydrogel particles. The volume fraction of particles is such that the mechanical properties of the material are intermediate between a very viscous fluid and a soft solid. Despite the non-Newtonian character of these hydrogels, we find that when the surface of the material is free, an immersed buoyant sphere rises with a constant speed. The effects of the motion in the surrounding material are observed to be highly localized around the sphere. When the mass of the sphere is varied, its terminal velocity is observed to depend exponentially on the buoyancy.  Qualitatively distinct behavior is found when a lid is present on the surface of the material, and in this case, a sublinear time dependence of the displacement is found. Linear motion of the sphere is accompanied by flow at the surface of the material whereas fluid movement is suppressed when a lid is applied. Other boundary condition variations are consistent with the perspective that constant speed motion is induced by the presence of a free boundary.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}

\begin{figure*}
\centering
{\includegraphics[width=10cm]{fig-intro.pdf}}
\caption{Sketch (left) and front view of the apparatus with time lapse imaging (right). The sample comprised $12.7$ g of dry hydrogel beads in $2$ Litres of triply boiled tap water. The hollow sphere is $40\pm0.01$mm diameter and weighed $3.47$ g. The sphere appears wider at the equator due to lensing effects from the round container. The images are captured at $40$ second intervals. The equidistant separation of the time lapse images of the sphere highlights its constant speed.}
\label{fig:intro}
\end{figure*} 


The use of a falling-bob viscometer to make  estimates of the viscosity of Newtonian fluids is a standard laboratory procedure. It involves applying  Stokes law for the motion past a sphere \citep{Stokes} to the terminal speed of rigid particles falling or rising in very viscous fluids or at very small lengthscales. Stokes law applies when $Re<<1$ where the Reynolds number $Re = \frac{ U r}{\nu}$ with $U$, the terminal speed of the  sphere of radius $r$ and $\nu$ is the kinematic viscosity of the fluid. Typically, the law is valid for many viscous oils and fluids such as glycerine and syrups. The falling bob viscometer technique is nowadays highly refined \citep{tang, patramanis} where effects from the walls of the container~\citep{ladenburg1907influence,wall} or the finite length of the apparatus~\citep{tanner} are taken into account. Moreover, this configuration has been used to investigate the motion of rigid spheres through suspensions of neutrally buoyant spheres over a range of volume fractions~\citep{susp} and suspensions of fine rods \citep{susp2}. The method has also been extended to falling cylinders in narrow tubes~\citep{PRUSA2012743}.

Typically, it is assumed that the fluid properties are  independent of time and speed for falling-bob viscometers to be used effectively. However, these techniques can also be used to provide deeper insights into the flow behavior of some non-Newtonian fluids, such as viscoelastic materials~\citep{nNexp}. A study using a shear thinning fluid found that the distance the sphere travels is $\propto t^{1/2}$ \citep{Mckinley}. An explanation for this non-linear behaviour is provided by \citep{beris1985creeping} who demonstrate that a build up of stagnant fluid around the sphere leads to an increase in its effective size and hence  Stokes drag increases with time. In shear thickening fluids, a similar coupling has been observed between the effective size of the intruder and the viscous response of the material surrounding it for the non-linear slowdown of an intruder~\citep{brassard2021viscous}. 

The focus of our current study is on a class of non-Newtonian materials, namely mixtures of hydrated hydrogel particles. Indicative of the ambiguous mechanical nature of these materials, we also refer to them as hydrogel  suspensions when we discuss their overall fluid-like properties, or hydrogel packing when we discuss the static properties of the sedimented hydrogel particles. They have many remarkable features, including sensitivity to the frictional properties of the hydrogels, non-Newtonian flow profiles, critical packing fraction behavior in their high flow rate rheology and various forms of creep behavior~\citep{workamp2019contact, PhysRevFluids.3.084303, gueslguazzelli, 2022creepcontrol, 2024creepconf}. Control of the properties of hydrogel suspensions in water can be achieved via the friction of hydrogel particles~\citep{workamp2019contact}, packing density~\citep{shewan2015viscosity, shewan2021viscoelasticity, gueslguazzelli, 2022creepcontrol} and even using the valency of added ions~\citep{budtova1994rheological}. In previous studies, the non-Newtonian fluid properties of dense hydrogel suspensions is paramount. For example, hydrogel suspensions have been shown to behave as Herschel-Bulkley fluids~\citep{workamp2019contact} when probed with standard rheological methods under steady state and constant driving rate conditions.

\subsection{Constant speed motion}
Our primary goal is to use the conceptually simple geometry of a falling-bob viscometer to investigate our counterintuitive observation that under some circumstances a buoyant, rising sphere  can travel at a constant speed through samples of hydrated hydrogel suspensions. Results are obtained  over a wide variety of sample conditions and sphere sizes and are surprising since hydrogel suspensions are materials with well-established non-Newtonian properties. Indeed, our previous work revealed a creep flow motion in which the speed typically decreases with the square root of time~\citep{2022creepcontrol,2024creepconf}, indicating the presence of significant non-Newtonian behavior at these  constant stress driving conditions. Hence, observation of constant speed motion motion over a wide parameter range is a new and surprising result.

In our investigations, we systematically vary the boundary condition of the hydrogel particle mixture to find that the difference between the constant speed motion observed in the current work and the nonlinear creep motion observed previously originates in the boundary conditions applied to the material. Despite the rheology of hydrogel suspensions being an intrinsic property of the microscopics of the material, we establish that its global mechanical response can be affected by the boundary conditions, which is highly unusual. Our work suggests that further theoretical work is required to describe the constitutive behaviour of hydrogel particle suspensions in the presence of specific boundary conditions.\\

\subsection{Dependence on material properties and boundary conditions}
The  motion of rigid intruders  is  described using concepts based on the observed  constant terminal speed of the spheres. We  confirm that the material flows in a continuous way and the intruder moves smoothly as resolved using  high speed video imaging. We use these observational facts to assign an effective constant viscosity $\eta_{\rm eff}$ at a prescribed value of the stress, even though it is clear that the material under investigation is non-Newtonian.  In fact, the  effective viscosity characterization of the material reveals an exponential stress dependence of this property on the buoyancy stress exerted by the intruder. This suggests Arrhenius or Eyring-type~\citep{eyring1935activated} mechanisms to be a leading factor in the dissipative mechanisms of the material, albeit that they occur at much lower stress levels than the traditional pressure sensitivity of ordinary viscous fluids~\citep{bridgman1925viscosity}. Even so, the exponential stress dependence is also observed in the non-Newtonian, time-dependent creep behavior previously observed in hydrogel suspensions~\citep{2022creepcontrol,2024creepconf}.\\ 
The dependence on temperature of the observed suspension flow behavior is observed to be significantly greater than the temperature dependence of water alone. This finding indicates  that the effective viscosity of the suspension is not dominated by the solvent viscosity alone e.g. via a local mechanism such as a lubrication layers between the particles, as these would scale sinking or rising speeds proportionally with the water viscosity. In fact, particle image velocimetry analyses reveal that the deformation field of the hydrogel suspension is highly localised around the intruder.\\
Despite the localization of the flow, we show that the constant speed motion is consistently changed to a sub-linear time dependence of the displacement when a buoyant sphere rises towards a rigid lid floating on the upper boundary of the hydrogel suspension. We therefore systematically explore different boundary conditions of the hydrogel suspension to find that constant speed motion is found for all intruder motions towards a free boundary, including a  boundary formed with a liquid on which the hydrogel suspension is floating.


\section{Experimental set-up}
A front view of the apparatus used in the majority of the experiments is shown in Fig.\ref{fig:intro}.  It comprises a $130$ mm o.d., $200$mm long  plexiglass cylinder which has a $3$mm thick wall. The cylinder is aligned vertically in the laboratory frame of reference and is mounted on  a $10$mm thick aluminium base which has a machined $129$mm diameter circular groove containing an 'O' ring. A silicone sealant is used to attach the cylinder to the base. The cylinder contains two litres of triply boiled Oxford tap water. Boiling is used to reduce the amount of  dissolved gases in the water (see for example \cite{degassing}) and hence help prevent the formation of bubbles over the periods of the experiment, which were typically months. It is observed that the presence of bubbles can significantly affect the reproducibility of the experimental results, probably as a result of the effect of compressibility and surface tension on the packing mechanics~\citep{2022creepcontrol} and the relatively high Laplace pressure in small bubbles in comparison to the other stress scales that apply in the material. Repeatable results are obtained over the entire period of the experiment using this sample preparation protocol.

\subsection{Upper Boundary Conditions}
The cylinder is sealed at the top by a tight fitting lid  containing a neoprene 'O' ring. The sealed container  helps to minimise any evaporation of the water. There is a $1$ cm gap between the upper surface of the sample and the underside of the external lid. The upper boundary of the sample is a free surface for $\sim 20\%$ of the experiments and a printed plastic lid is placed on the surface of the sample in the remainder of the experiments. The lid is  3D printed in plastic (PLA), hence it is partially aerated and weighs $\sim  50.3$ g. Its bottom surface is smooth since it is formed in contact with the heating element of the 3D printer, and is hence comparatively free of defects. It is placed on the surface of the hydrogel packing and a thin layer of water is squeezed  on onto the upper surface. The lid is lightweight and the results of an investigation into the effects of confinement \citep{2024creepconf} indicate its contribution to the bulk stress on the material is minimal. 

Finally, a third upper boundary condition is imposed using a steel mesh  placed on the surface of the sample. The stainless steel mesh was circular and contained an array of approximately square holes formed by $0.5$mm wires which cross orthogonally. Meshes of this type are used in devices such as cafetieres. It has a diameter of $100$~mm, thickness $1.0$mm and a pore size of $0.2$mm and weighs $\approx 3.6$ g. Hence water may pass through the mesh easily, while hydrogels are held in position by the mesh. The surface of the mesh is  very rough, providing a no-slip boundary condition to the hydrogels.

A time lapse set of images are superposed on the frontview of the apparatus in Fig. \ref{fig:intro} to give an impression of the upward motion of a ping-pong ball. To initiate the experiment a buoyant ping pong ball is pushed down to the centre of the base of the cylinder using a $1$ mm diameter metal rod which has a hoop at one end. Smaller spheres were manipulated with a similarly shaped $0.5$ mm diameter rod. A settling period of $10$ minutes is allowed before the ball is  released on the centre line  and the hoop is carefully moved to the side  of the cylinder. The timescale for the insertion and release of the sphere are typically much shorter than the transit time through the sample.

\subsection{Particle tracking}
The motion of the sphere is tracked by capturing images using a computer controlled  camera at set intervals ranging from $5$ to $100$ seconds depending on the buoyancy of the ball. In the case shown in Fig.\ref{fig:intro}  the images are taken at $40$ second intervals using a Nikon $400$ digital camera. The recorded images are stored on a PC using the software DigiCam and are processed offline using the software ImageJ. The speed is estimated from the resulting distance versus time plots.

\subsection{Sample preparation}
Sample preparation proceeds as follows.  Dry hydrogel powder is slowly mixed with the water which is gently stirred and left to settle for two hours between additions. The particles swell as they absorb water and  sink to the bottom of the cylinder as they are slightly more dense than the fluid. The sample is gently stirred between each charge. The hydrogels absorb the water efficiently and form a layer of swollen particles at the bottom of the cylinder with a clear layer of fluid above. Measurements of the depth of the hydrogel layer as a function of the added mass of dry powder is linear as in previous experiments~\citep{2022creepcontrol}. This finding is consistent with the hydrogel particles being essentially frictionless spheres~\citep{gong2006friction, workamp2019contact, Shakya2024}, which settle to a random close packing.  The hydrogel particle packing reaches the surface of the water at a dry hydrogel content $\phi \approx 6$ g/L where $\phi$ is defined as the weight of the dry powder divided by the total volume of water in grams per Litre (g/L).  Once the layer has reached the surface, the addition of further quantities of dry hydrogels does not give any detectable change in the layer height and presumably there is a small compression of the particles throughout the layer. The average size of the swollen particles is measured to be $\sim 1$ mm. The value of $\phi$ used in the majority of the experiments reported here is set to $6.35$ g/L. 

The experiments with rising spheres were performed over a period of approximately one year in a laboratory constructed in TM's home which is a well-insulated property with a thatched roof. This provides a good short-term stable temperature environment. The temperature of the sample is recorded at regular intervals and for the majority of the period it is found to be $19.0 \pm 1.0$ °C. Over the summer months the temperature varied outside of this range and the effects of this are reported below. However, the majority of the data given below is collected with the temperature within the given range. We will see below that this temperature window gives at most a $10\%$ variation of measured travelling speeds, which is insignificant for the effects considered, given the large range of travelling speeds explored via the variation of buoyancy stress and intruder size.


Experiments were also performed in a laboratory environment at the University of Amsterdam, under standard laboratory temperature control. In these cases  a suspension of hydrogel spheres was formed in a square plexiglass box of dimensions $25\times25\times40$~cm following our established protocol using dry hydrogel spheres with triply boiled Amsterdam tap water at a density of $6.09$ g/L unless otherwise noted. To initiate the experiments, a rod with a hoop was mainly used to place the sphere at the bottom, and in other cases a small magnet was placed inside the ball. An external magnet was used to hold and release the ping pong ball from the bottom of the container. 

In the Amsterdam experiments the software TroublePix was used with a Lumenera Lt225C high speed camera to record a video of the motion at a frame rate of $71$ frames/second. Still images were captured using a Nikon D5200 camera with ball tracking  imaging performed using  MaxTraq and Matlab. The flow field was measured using Particle Image Velocimetry (PIV). In this, the plane of motion of the  sphere was illuminated by two vertical green laser sheets. Spatial averaging which co-moves with the intruder was used. Matlab integrated software ``PIVlab'' was used to analyse the sequences of images. 

\subsection{Intruder specifications}
Ping pong balls are manufactured from Acrylonitrile Butadiene Styrene (ABS) plastic, are $40\pm0.1 $mm in diameter and weigh $ 2.73\pm  0.2$ g. Their size, sphericity and weight are strictly controlled by the International Table Tennis Federation~\citep{ITTFballstandards} and the dimensions of  each ball are checked using a vernier gauge. The diameter ratio of the sphere to container $ d/D \approx 0.32 $ where $d$ is the diameter of the sphere and $D$ is that of the cylinder. Changing the buoyancy of a ping-pong ball is relatively straightforward as water can be injected into the ball using a hypodermic needle, the injection hole sealed and the weight of the ball measured using an electronic balance. The buoyancy force can then be estimated using the difference in mass between the sphere and the displaced water. We assume that the stress $\sigma_S$ that an intruder exerts on the hydrogel suspension is its effective weight divided by its projected cross sectional area. 
The main set of experiments were performed using ping-pong balls with a hydrogel sample of 6.35 g/L.  A second set of experiments were carried out using a hollow 20mm diameter polypropylene ball (Precision plastic ball co.). Injecting water and weighing was again used to change and estimate the buoyancy. The value of $\phi$ was 6.0 g/L. A third set of experiments were performed with hollow plastic balls. The balls again came from the Precision Ball Co., were made from a range of plastics (polyamide, polypropylene, PTFE, polyethylene) and had diameters $25.0, 20.0, 19.0$mm. These hollow plastic balls were weighed using an electronic balance and the  the weight was used to compute the buoyancy stress. The value of $\phi$ used was 6.15 g/L for these experiments. Finally, in one experiment, a glass sphere with a diameter of 25mm was used.

