\section{Overall rising intruder dynamics}                        

The indicative yet informative and simple result of an initial set of experiments is shown in Fig.~\ref{resover} 
where it can be seen from a single typical experimental data set that the distance a sphere rises in a hydrogel sample is proportional to $t$ when there is a free surface at the top of the sample. The inset Fig.~\ref{resover} shows the linearity of the displacement dynamics also on a double logarithmic scale.\\ The constant speed motion  stands in stark contrast with previous square-root time dependence observations~\citep{2022creepcontrol, 2024creepconf}. From the systematic exploration of intruder motion as reported below, we will see that the (end) boundary plays a defining role in the timescale of the motion of the sphere through the sample. We first present a systematic investigation into this constant speed motion of buoyant spheres moving towards a free boundary.

