\subsection{Time Histories with a Free Surface}
\label{subsec:timehistory}

As discussed above, the buoyancy of the ping-pong ball can readily be changed by adding small measured quantities of water to its interior. The time histories obtained over a range of buoyancies are shown in Fig. \ref{fig:risinglinear} (a). It can be seen that a straight line provides a satisfactory least squares fit to each time history with typically only a 3\% root mean squared error, and hence there is a linear dependence of distance on time. A wide range of buoyancies was explored using this approach, from a sphere without added water ($\sigma_S =240$Pa) and the total rise time is $\sim 2$ minutes to one with $\sigma_S = 130$ Pa, where the sphere takes $\sim 2$ hours to rise through the sample. We show all the $\delta(t)$ behaviour for the experiments with ping-pong balls in a sample with $\phi=6.35$ g/L and a free upper surface in Fig.~\ref{fig:risinglinear} (a). 
A fixed terminal velocity is found for each value of $\sigma_S$ in the specified sample. This suggests an interpretation of the results using the laws of standard Stokes viscometry might be possible. Of course, the connection cannot be complete since we shall see that the rise velocity depends exponentially on buoyancy; the hydrogel sample  has clear non-Newtonian properties. Even so, models of rigid particles in suspensions \citep{Cheng} suggest that an effective viscosity can be ascribed to suspensions of particles in fluids. Following this approach we extract an effective viscosity of the hydrogel medium from the constant intruder velocity data with 

\[
\eta_{\rm eff} =\frac{2\Delta\rho R^2g}{9U}, \label{eq:effvisc}
\]

\noindent where $\eta_{\rm eff}$ is the effective dynamic viscosity of the fluid, $R$ is the radius of the sphere, $\Delta\rho = \rho_I-\rho_h$ is the difference in density between the intruder and hydrogel medium respectively, $g$ is gravity and $U$ is the terminal velocity of the sphere. Strictly speaking, this formula is only valid for a Stokes flow in an unbounded domain. If the fluid medium used here was a Newtonian viscous fluid, wall and end effects would be significant for the geometry used here. However, as was found in~\citep{kudrollihydrogel} and reported below, the hydrogel suspension motion is only significant close to the sphere, so we choose the simplified analysis for the present study. We will show PIV measurements below which also indicate that the flow field is localised around the sphere.\\
The effective viscosity approach allows us to rescale all the acquired displacement data for one sample onto a master curve that follows $\delta \propto t$. We show the result of this analysis in Fig.~\ref{fig:risinglinear} (b). An excellent collapse over three orders of magnitude confirms the constant speed dynamics of an intruder rising towards a free surface.

The collapse of the data provides an important message. It is possible that the time dependence observed previously~\citep{2022creepcontrol,2024creepconf} could arise from a gradient in the properties of the sample resulting from concentration variations or the effects of  hydrostatic pressure, for example, or other gradients because of  the sensitivity of the viscosity to packing density around the random close packing limit~\citep{shewan2015viscosity}. The constant speed motion, instead, highlights that concentrated samples of hydrated hydrogels which do not display spatiotemporal density variation effects can be consistently produced.

A graph of the effective viscosities of the time histories presented in Fig.~\ref{fig:risinglinear} (a), (b) versus buoyancy stress $\sigma_S$ is given in Fig.~\ref{fig:risinglinear} (c). The dependence is exponential as shown by the linear fit to the data since the ordinate has a logarithmic scale. Hence, $U~\propto~\exp(-\sigma_S/\sigma_0)$ with $\sigma_0$ a constant that depends on the hydrogel density, among other factors. This exponential dependence of propagation speeds also depends on packing fraction \citep{2022creepcontrol} and global confinement stress~\citep{2024creepconf}, which is consistent with material response previously reported for these materials.

