\section{End boundary conditions}
All the data we have discussed thus far displays a linear dependence of distance on time for spheres moving towards a free boundary. As discussed in the introduction, previous results for the motion of heavy spheres through hydrated hydrogels ~\citep{2022creepcontrol,2024creepconf} which correspond to cumulative 200+ measurements show $d \propto t^{1/2}$. The main difference between the two configurations is that in the present study, the ping-pong balls rise towards a free surface  whereas the heavy spheres and cylinders used in earlier work were descending towards  a rigid boundary. A question which may be asked is whether the approached end boundary condition plays a role in the motion of the intruder. 

We therefore compare the example intruder motion data from Fig.~\ref{fig:intro} obtained from an intruder moving towards a free boundary, with observations of the same intruder motion moving in the  same hydrogel sample towards a light rigid lid floating on the hydrogel sample. We observe that the motion of the buoyant sphere moving towards the floating lid is clearly sub-linear. The example data for sub-linear motion shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:sublinear} is best fitted with a power law of $\delta \propto t^{\alpha}$ with $\alpha = 0.73$, as can be seen in the double logarithmic inset in Fig.~\ref{fig:sublinear} .  

After systematically exploring this positive buoyancy case for a hydrogel suspension confined between two solid boundaries (a lid at the hydrogel surface and the container wall at the bottom), we also investigate  two other boundary conditions. Firstly, we  measure the motion of an intruder with a positive buoyancy and a packing confined by a stiff, porous metal mesh. Secondly, we investigate the motion of intruders with both positive and negative  buoyancies with a hydrogel sample confined between two fluid boundaries (air at the top, heavy liquid at the bottom). Finally, we consider flow field aspects that pertain to the boundary conditions, visualizing the strength of the displacement field close to the boundary at the top of an unconstrained hydrogel packing. These additional observations force us to conclude that the end boundary conditions play a crucial role in setting the overall flow behaviour of intruders in the hydrogel packing.

\begin{figure*}
\centering
{\includegraphics[clip, trim=0.5cm 3.5cm 0.5cm 3cm,scale=0.7]{10-checknl1.pdf}}
\caption{(a) Time histories of  ping pong balls with different buoyancies rising through a hydrogel sample towards a rigid lid. The  sample used was the same as used to obtain data for Fig.~\ref{fig:risinglinear}. The nonlinear data is fitted with the  function $t^{\alpha}$ with $\alpha < 1$. (b) rescaling $(\delta(t)/\eta_{\rm eff})^{1/\alpha}$. The dash-dotted line has slope 1. (c) $\eta_{\rm eff}$ depends exponentially on $\sigma_S$, as indicated by the dash-dotted line. The line represents the same function as used in Fig.~\ref{fig:risinglinear}b.}
\label{fig:risingnonlinear}
\end{figure*}

\subsection{Sphere moving towards a rigid floating lid.}

To systematically address the issue of the effects of end boundary conditions, a sequence of experiments are performed where a printed plastic lid  sits on top of the swollen hydrogels.  The sample used as before has $\phi=6.35$ g/L and the buoyancy of the sphere is adjusted by adding or subtracting water to and from the interior of the ping-pong ball as described previously. 

As outlined above in the discussion of the results in Fig.~\ref{fig:intro}, the qualitative difference in the time series plots for cases with and without a lid is clear. At the same value of $\phi$ and buoyancy the sphere rises with a linear distance-time relationship with a free surface and is sub-linear, tending towards $t^{1/2}$, when the lid is present. Note that a dependence of distance travelled on $\sqrt{t}$ is qualitatively different to the end effects in falling-bob experiments on viscous Newtonian flows \citep{tanner}.  

Thus, we systematically explore the effects  of a lid on $\delta(t)$ behavior. The results of tests with ping pong balls of various weights are shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:risingnonlinear} (a). As shown in the example data from Fig.~\ref{fig:sublinear}, we observe a clear sublinear time-dependent behavior of the displacement of the sphere. To extract the time dependence of the displacement of the rising sphere, we again fit to each data set 
\[
\delta(t) = \frac{t^\alpha}{\eta_{\rm eff}} \label{eq:nonlin}
\]
Here, $\eta_{\rm eff}$ is again a prefactor that sets the speed of motion. Despite the non-Newtonian nature of the flow behavior, we refer to it as the effective viscosity. We can  verify which rescaling coefficient provides the best collapse of the data; plotting $(\delta\eta_{\rm eff})^{1/\alpha}(t)$  results in a linear time dependence. We find that $\alpha = 0.75$ provides the best collapse of the data and show this result in Fig.~\ref{fig:risingnonlinear}b.  We verify the exponential dependence of prefactor $\eta_{\rm eff}$ that we use to characterize the strength of the sublinear behavior in Fig.~\ref{fig:risingnonlinear}c. Since the same hydrogel sample was used here, we can compare the exponential dependence on the buoyancy stress for the sublinear case to the case of the rising sphere towards the free surface as discussed in Sec.~\ref{subsec:timehistory} (dash-dotted line). The data for the sublinear case clearly follows the same scaling of $\eta_{\rm eff} \propto \exp(-\sigma_S/\sigma_0)$, with the same $\sigma_0 = 27$ Pa as used for the free surface case.  We conclude that motion of a sphere towards a rigid boundary is sublinear by way of contrast with the motion towards a free surface, which is linear.

\subsection{Sphere moving towards a rigid base --- comparison with previous work}.

In previous work~\citep{2022creepcontrol,2024creepconf} we performed experiments on sinking intruders that moved towards  the rigid base of the container. These additional data enable us to compare the observed time dependence with the cases discussed in this manuscript. We extract the data from the open repositories, e.g.~\citep{data_SM} and perform the  same fitting procedure to estimate the $\delta(t)$ behavior for these data sets.\\ 
We can extract the exponent for which a power law model $\delta(t) = at^{\alpha} + c$ provides the best fit. We note that there is no canonical definition of a ``best'' fit, as fit quality as measured by a reduced $\chi^2$ depends also on weights associated to data, and even with an optimal $\chi^2$, systematic deviations in the residual of the fit are neglected. Our choice for ``best'' is hence simply the best goodness of fit estimate as returned by the MATLAB-based fitting routine.\\ 
Applying this same standard across all data sets from two previous publications and the current one, yields the results as shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:exponentstats}, with over 300 $\delta(t)$ sets fitted. The statistics of the distribtion of $\alpha$ for each case of ``rigid'' boundary, ``floating lid'' and ``free'' surface is indicated with a violin plot, including a separate scatter plot for the mean and standard deviation of the distribution (error bar). We observe a clear clustering of exponents around 0.5 for all the previously published data, in which an intruder was sinking towards a rigid base. In the current study, some data was obtained with an intruder rising towards a rigid lid floating on the hydrogel packing; the exponents for these experiments center around 0.7. The exponents for the creeping flow measurements for intruders that move towards a free boundary clearly center around $\alpha=1$, confirming again the strong influence of the boundary on the flow behavior of the hydrogel suspension. Lastly, for the data sets of the previous works we can obtain a satisfactory collapse as obtained for the current data as shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:risingnonlinear} (b). The temporal resolution in our previous work was lower than used here, but the exponent which provides a satisfactory collapse for these datasets is $0.5$ for data from ~\citep{2022creepcontrol,2024creepconf} albeit with increased scatter. All data and analysis scripts for all our work can be found in open data repositories~\citep{data_SM}.

