\subsection {Rising towards a surface with a mesh}
We show in Fig.~\ref{fig:mesh} four time histories with hydrogel density $\phi =6.15$ g/L. Two of the time histories were measured with a free surface and two with a metal mesh which sits $\sim 1$mm below the surface of the packing. The mesh prevents the hydrogel particles at the surface from moving during the transit of the ping-pong ball, yet it is sufficiently porous that  water will flow relatively unimpeded. This boundary condition can  is rough and hence suppresses vertical and  radial motion of the hydrogels  at the top of the  packing. However, the mesh is also very light.  The results indicate that the mesh has very little effect on the constant speed motion of the ping-pong ball. Moreover, the surface of the sample remains flat during the motion of the ball, suggesting that the constant speed motion is not an end effect imposed by having a surface structure imposed, suggesting that flexible rough lids would also not create sublinear motion. We cannot exclude the possibility that a smooth yet flexible boundary such as the surface tension boundary conditions from~\cite{2022creepcontrol} can nevertheless be essential to create sublinear motion.

