\subsection{Rising towards a free surface: radial displacement field }
To investigate the radial velocity profile of the hydrogel suspension during a rising sphere test, the hydrogel suspension is marked with some coloured swollen hydrogel beads, and their motion is imaged from the top of the container. The image shown in the top right panel of Fig. \ref{fig:flowstruct-boundary} is a composite constructed from $32$ still images taken at $10$ second intervals during the period in which the sphere is moving towards the surface. Evidence for outward motion from the centre of the ball is clear. Upward movement of the hydrogel surface was also observed; see Fig. \ref{fig:flowstruct-boundary}, bottom right.  The flow visualisation  observations in Fig. \ref{fig:flowstruct-boundary} illustrate that the radial flow of the hydrogel suspension above the sphere is substantial, and that boundary of the hydrogel fluid can deform significantly. However, as indicated with the mesh experiments, these deformation fields alone do not seem to be responsible for creating (sub)linear time dependent motion of the intruder.



\section{Conclusions}

We performed falling-ball viscometry tests of hydrogel suspensions. We used  hollow plastic spheres to observe the dynamics of a rising sphere as a function of time. The spheres rise with $\delta \propto t$ in cases with a free surface on the hydrogel suspension. Each run of the experiment has this feature. The ascent rate is found to depend exponentially on the buoyancy stress i.e. $\propto \exp{(\sigma_S)}$ and therefore there is clear evidence for non-Newtonian behavior in the hydrogel suspension. We confirm the non-Newtonian behavior with temperature tests and flow field visualization, both of which reveal non-Stokesian dynamics. We contrast the linear rise dynamics with creep flow found earlier to confirm a dependence of the flow behavior on the boundary conditions. An intruder sinking towards a free boundary also shows linear displacement dynamics, and we find that when a freely floating lid is placed on the surface of the material, $\delta =  t^{\alpha}/\eta_{\rm eff}$, with $\alpha \neq 1$. The qualitative difference between the two types of motion is accompanied by clear evidence of flow at the free surface. The consistency of the results indicates that theoretical progress may be possible using continuum models in these flows that include boundary conditions as an effective way to tune the rheology of the bulk material.

