\title{When are exact categories co-exact?}
\author{J. R. A. Gray}
\begin{document}
\maketitle
\begin{abstract}
We show that opposite category
of a cocomplete pretopos is ideally exact and arithmetical.
We do this by showing that there are conditions common to additive
and lextensive categories which, in the
exact context, imply that: pushouts of monomorphisms along arbitrary
morphisms exist and are pullbacks; monomorphisms are stable under pushout;
co-reflexive-relations are co-effective-equivalence-relations; and
coprotomodularity holds.
\end{abstract}
\section{Introduction}
This paper is written in part as an invitation to further research into the
question posed in the title.
The Tierney ``equation''
\[(\text{Barr-exact}) + (\text{additive})=(\text{abelian}) \]
immediately gives an answer to this question,
since the opposite category of an abelian category is abelian. On,e
of the main aims of this paper is to explain that extensivity is also an answer
to the question (subject to the existence of certain colimits), so that every
finitely cocomplete pretopos is co-exact, and also to explain that there
is a condition generalizing both extensivity and 
additivity which together with exactness implies co-exactness (provided certain
colimits exist). In addition to this we show
that every finitely cocomplete pretopos is co-ideally-exact and co-arithmetical,
and that our above mentioned condition also implies coprotomodularity.
The former result recovers as a special case Theorem 1.3 of \cite{BORCEUX_CLEMENTINO:2025} 
showing that the opposite category of pointed Hausdorff spaces is semi-abelian.

Let us begin by recalling the necessary background as well as
introducing the above mentioned condition so that we can
explain more precisely what our aims are. 
A category $\C$ is regular \cite{BARR:1971} if it is finitely complete, admits
coequalizers of kernel pairs, and the class of regular epimorphisms is pullback
stable in it. A regular category $\C$ is called exact \cite{BARR:1971} when equivalence relations are effective,
that is, (the projections of) (internal) equivalence
relations are kernel pairs in it. A category $\C$ is extensive \cite{CARBONI_LACK_WALTERS:1993} if it admits binary coproducts and 
for each pair of objects $A$ and $B$ the coproduct functor
\begin{equation}
	\tag{\#}
	\label{extensive_equiv}
\vcenter{
\xymatrix@R=0.5ex{
	(\C\downarrow A)\times (\C\downarrow B) \ar[r] & (\C\downarrow A+B)\\
	((X,f),(Y,g)) \ar@{|->}[r]\ar[dddd]_{(u,v)} & (X+Y,f+g)\ar[dddd]^{u+v}\\
\\ \phantom{aaaa}\ar@{|->}[r] & \phantom{aaaa}\\ \\
	((X',f'),(Y',g')) \ar@{|->}[r] & (X'+Y',f+g')
}
}
\end{equation}
is an equivalence of categories. This turns out to be equivalent to 
$\C$ having binary coproducts, pullbacks of coproduct
	inclusions along arbitrary morphisms existing in $\C$, and for each diagram
\[
\xymatrix{
	A \ar[r]^{i}\ar[d]^{f} & C\ar@{}[dl]|{(3)}\ar@{}[dr]|{(4)}\ar[d]^{h} & B\ar[l]_{j}\ar[d]^{g} & \text{\scriptsize{(1)}}\\
A '\ar[r]_{i'} & C & B',\ar[l]^{j'} & \text{\scriptsize{(2)}}
}
\]
in $\C$,
where (2) is a coproduct, (1) is a coproduct if and only if (3) and (4) are pullbacks.
A category is a pretopos if it is exact and extensive. A category with binary products
is additive when each hom-set is equipped with an abelian group structure which is 
bilinear with respect to composition. A category is abelian when it admits products, coproducts,
kernels and cokernels, and every monomorphism is a normal monomorphism and every epimorphism
is a normal epimorphism. Recall that for an object $B$ the category $\Pt_{\C}(B)$ has objects
triples $(A,\alpha,\beta)$ where $A$ is an object in $\C$ and $\alpha: A\to B$ and $\beta : B\to A$
are morphisms in $\C$ such that $\alpha \beta=1_B$. A morphism
$f:(A,\alpha,\beta)\to (A',\alpha',\beta')$ in $\Pt_{\C}(B)$ is a morphism $f:A\to A'$ such that 
$\alpha' f =  \alpha$ and $f\beta =\beta'$. Under the assumption that pullbacks of split
epimorphisms along arbitrary morphisms exist, each morphism $p:E\to B$ determines a pullback functor
$p^* : \Pt_{\C}(B) \to \Pt_{\C}(E)$ , which sends an object $(A,\alpha,\beta)$ to 
$(E\times_B A,\pi_1,\langle 1,\beta p\rangle)$ where $(E\times_B A,\pi_1,\pi_2)$ is the pullback $p$ and $\alpha$, and $\langle 1,\beta p\rangle$ is the unique morphism such that
$\pi_1\langle 1,\beta p\rangle=1_E$ and $\pi_2\langle 1,\beta p\rangle =\beta p$.
A category is protomodular \cite{BOURN:1991} if pullbacks of split epimorphisms along arbitrary morphisms
exist and each functor $p^*$ reflects isomorphisms. It is well-known that when $\C$ has
finite limits and an initial object, protomodularity is equivalent to requiring for each morphism $p:0\to B$ the functor $p^{*}$ reflects isomorphisms for monomorphisms. A category
is ideally exact \cite{JANELIDZE:2024} if it is exact, protomodular, finitely cocomplete and the
unique morphism $0\to 1$ is a regular epimorphism.

For a category $\C$ we consider the following condition.
\begin{condition}
\label{conditions}
\begin{enumerate}[(a)]
\item binary coproducts exist in $\C$;
\item binary coproducts of pullback squares are pullback squares in $\C$;
\item For each monomorphism $m: S\to A$ in $\C$ the square
\[
\xymatrix{
S+S \ar[d]_{[1_S,1_A]} \ar[r]^{m+1_S} & A+S\ar[d]^{[1_A,m]}\\
S \ar[r]_{m} & A
}
\]
is a pullback;
\item for each regular epimorphism $p:E\to B$ in $\C$, the diagram
\[
\xymatrix{
E+E \ar[d]_{[1_E,1_E]} \ar[r]^{p+p} & B+B\ar[d]^{[1_B,1_B]}\\
E \ar[r]_{p} & B
}
\]
is a feeble pullback, by which we mean that the unique morphism into the pullback is a regular
epimorphism.
\end{enumerate}
\end{condition}
The main objectives of the paper are 
(i) to show that Condition \ref{conditions} 
holds for any additive category as well as for any lextensive category;
(ii) to show that any exact category $\C$ satisfying Condition \ref{conditions}
has the following properties:
\begin{enumerate}
\item pushouts of monomorphisms exist in $\C$ and are pullbacks;
\item monomorphisms are stable under pushout in $\C$;
\item monomorphism are regular monomorphisms in $\C$ and hence $\C$ is balanced;
\item co-reflexive-relations in $\C$ are co-effective-equivalence-relations;
\item $\C$ is coprotomodular. 
\end{enumerate}
\section{Lextensive categories}
In this section we prove that lextensive categories satisfy Condition \ref{conditions}.
In order to do so we need a few preliminary results.
\begin{lemma}\label{lemma:1}
For each morphism $f:A \to B$ in a lextensive category the diagram
\[
\xymatrix{
A + A \ar[r]^-{[1_A,1_A]}\ar[d]_{f+f} & A\ar[d]^{f}\\
B+B\ar[r]_-{[1_B,1_B]} & B
}
\]
is a pullback.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Consider the diagram 
\[
\xymatrix{
	A \ar[d]_{f}\ar[r]^{i_s}\ar@/^3ex/[rr]^{1_A} & P \ar[r]^{q}\ar[d]_{p} & A\ar[d]^{f} \\
	B \ar[r]_{\iota_s} & B+B \ar[r]_{[1_B,1_B]} & B
}
\]
where the right hand square is a pullback and $i_s$ is the unique morphism making the
	diagram commute ($s\in \{1,2\}$). By extensivity we know that $(P,i_1,i_2)$ is a coproduct, and hence
since $p i_s=\iota_s f$ and $q i_s = 1_A$ the claim follows.
\end{proof}
Since the functors \eqref{extensive_equiv} are equivalences it follows that:
\begin{lemma}\label{lemma:2}
	Binary coproducts of pullbacks are pullbacks in an extensive category.
\end{lemma}
\begin{lemma}\label{lemma:3}
	For morphisms $f:A\to X$, $g:B\to X$, $h:C\to X$ in a
	lextensive category, the diagram
	\[
		\xymatrix@C=10ex{
			P+R \ar[r]^-{[p_2,r_2]} \ar[d]_{p_1+r_1} & C\ar[d]^{h}\\
			A+B\ar[r]_{[f,g]} & X
		}
	\]
	is a pullback,
	where $(P,p_1,p_2)$ and  $(R,r_1,r_2)$ are
	pullbacks of $f$ and $h$, and $g$ and $h$,
	respectively.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
	According to Lemmas \ref{lemma:1} and \ref{lemma:2} it follows that each of the squares in the diagram
	\[
		\xymatrix{
			P+R \ar[r]^-{p_2+r_2}\ar[d]_{p_1+r_1}& C+C \ar[r]^-{[1_{C},1_{C}]}\ar[d]^{h+h} & C\ar[d]^{h}\\
			A+B \ar[r]_{f+g}&X+X \ar[r]_{[1_X,1_X]} & X
		}
	\]
	is a pullback and hence so is the desired diagram.
\end{proof}
As an immediate consequence we obtain:
\begin{lemma}\label{lemma:3.5}
For a monomorphism $m:S\to A$ in a
lextensive category, the diagram
\[
\xymatrix@C=10ex{
S+S \ar[r]^-{[1_S,1_S]} \ar[d]_{m+1_S} & S\ar[d]^{m}\\
A+S\ar[r]_{[1,m]} & A
}
\]
is a pullback.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proposition}
Every lextensive category satisfies Condition \ref{conditions}. 
\end{proposition}
\section{Additive categories}
In this section we prove that additive categories satisfy Condition \ref{conditions}.
	Note that in
this section we will use $\oplus$ to denote coproduct (which is also product) and reserve $+$ and $-$
for addition and subtraction of morphisms.
\begin{lemma}
Let $\C$ be an additive category. For each monomorphism $m:S\to A$ the diagram 
\[
\xymatrix@C=10ex{
S\oplus S \ar[r]^-{[1_S,1_S]} \ar[d]_{m\oplus 1_S} & S\ar[d]^{m}\\
A\oplus S\ar[r]_{[1,m]} & A
}
\]
is a pullback.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Suppose that $u:W\to S$ and $v=\langle v_1, v_2\rangle : W \to A\oplus S$ are morphisms
such that $[1,m]v=mu$. This means that $v_1 + mv_2=mu$ and hence
$v_1 = m(u-v_2)$. It follows that $(m\oplus 1_S)\langle u-v_2,v_2\rangle = \langle v_1,v_2\rangle$
and $[1_S,1_S]\langle u-v_2,v_2\rangle=u$. Uniqueness follows from the fact that $m \oplus 1_S$ is a monomorphism.
\end{proof}
\begin{lemma}
Let $\C$ be an additive category. For each regular epimorphism $f:A\to B$ the diagram 
\[
\xymatrix{
A \oplus A \ar[r]^-{[1_A,1_A]}\ar[d]_{f\oplus f} & A\ar[d]^{f}\\
B\oplus B\ar[r]_-{[1_B,1_B]} & B
}
\]
is a feeble pullback.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Just note that 
\[
\xymatrix{
	A \oplus B \ar[r]^-{[1_A,0]}\ar[d]_{\langle \iota_1-\iota_2,\iota_2\rangle (f\oplus 1_B)} & A\ar[d]^{f}\\
B\oplus B\ar[r]_-{[1_B,1_B]} & B
}
\]
	is a pullback, and $(1_A\oplus f) \langle \iota_1+\iota_2,\iota_2\rangle$ is the induced morphism (which is a regular epimorphism since it is the composite of a regular epimorphism and an
isomorphism).
\end{proof}
Since binary coproduct and product coincide and the binary product of two pullbacks is necessarily
a pullback it follows that:
\begin{lemma}
Binary coproducts of pullbacks are pullbacks in an additive category.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proposition}
Every additive category satisfies Condition \ref{conditions}.
\end{proposition}
\section{The main results}
In this section we prove our main results. We start with a few facts about feeble pullbacks.
As mentioned above in a special case, by a feeble pullback, we mean a commutative square
\begin{equation}
\label{star}
\tag{*}
\vcenter{
\xymatrix{
A\ar[d]_{\alpha} \ar[r]^{f} & B \ar[d]^{\beta}\\
A'\ar[r]^{f'} & B' 
}
}
\end{equation}
such that the induced morphism $A\to A'\times_{\langle f',\beta \rangle} B$
into the pullback is a regular epimorphism.
\begin{lemma}\label{feeble_is_pullback}
If \eqref{star}
is a feeble pullback in a finitely complete category and $\alpha$ and $f$ are jointly monomorphic, then
it is a pullback.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Just note that $\alpha$ and $f$ being jointly monomorphic force the induced morphism into the
pullback to be a monomorphism while being a feeble pullback forces it to be a regular epimorphism.
\end{proof}
\begin{lemma}\label{feeble_composite}
For a diagram
\[
\xymatrix{
A\ar[d]_{\alpha} \ar[r]^{f}\ar@{}[dr]|{\tiny\fbox{1}} & B \ar[d]^{\beta} \ar[r]^{g}\ar@{}[dr]|{\tiny\fbox{2}} & C \ar[d]^{\gamma}\\
A'\ar[r]_{f'} & B' \ar[r]_{g'} & C' 
}
\]
in a regular category $\C$ we have:
\begin{enumerate}[(a)]
\item if $\fbox{1}$ and $\fbox{2}$ are feeble pullbacks, then so is $\fbox{1}\fbox{2}$; 
\item if $\fbox{2}$ is a pullback, then $\fbox{1}$ is a feeble pullback if and only if $\fbox{1}\fbox{2}$ is a feeble pullback; 
\item if $\fbox{1}\fbox{2}$ is a feeble pullback and $f'$ is a 
regular epimorphism, then so is $\fbox{2}$; 
\end{enumerate}
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Consider the diagram
\[
\xymatrix@C=2ex@R=2ex{
	A\ar[ddddd]_{\alpha} \ar[rrrrr]^{f}\ar[dr]^{i}&&&&& B\ar[ddrr]^{j} \ar[ddddd]^{\beta} \ar[rrrrr]^{g} &&&&& C \ar[ddddd]^{\gamma}\\
	&P \ar[rrrru]^{p_2}\ar[dr]_{p_1}\ar@{}[drrrr]|{\tiny\fbox{5}}&&&& &&&&&\\
	&&Q \ar[rrrrr]^{q_2}\ar[dddll]^{q_1}\ar@{}[dddrrr]|{\tiny\fbox{4}}&&& &&R \ar[uurrr]^{r_2}\ar[dddll]^{r_1}\ar@{}[dddrrr]|{\tiny\fbox{3}}&&& \\
	&&&&& &&&&& \\
	&&&&& &&&&& \\
	A'\ar[rrrrr]_{f'} &&&&& B' \ar[rrrrr]_{g'} &&&&& C' 
} 
\]
where $\fbox{3}$, $\fbox{4}$ and $\fbox{5}$ are pullbacks, and $i$ and $j$ the
unique morphisms making the diagram commute. 
To prove (a) suppose $\fbox{1}$ and $\fbox{2}$ are feeble pullbacks.
It follows that $i$ and $j$ are regular
epimorphisms and hence by regularity so is $p_1$. Since regular epimorphisms
are closed under composition in a regular category, it follows that 
$p_1i$ is also a regular epimorphism. To prove (b) suppose
that $j$ is an isomorphism and note that since $p_1$ is then also an
isomorphism $p_1i$ is a regular epimorphism if and only if $i$ is. 
On the other hand to prove (c), suppose that
$\fbox{1}\fbox{2}$ is a feeble pullback and $f'$ is a 
regular epimorphism. It follows that that $q_2$ and $p_1i$ 
	are a regular epimorphisms. This means that $q_2p_1i=jp_2i$ is a regular
and hence that $j$ is a regular epimorphism as desired.
\end{proof}
\begin{remark}
It seems worth pointing out that there are several candidate definitions
for \emph{feeble limit} which do not require the existence of limits. 
One such is, given a functor $F:\X\to \C$, to define a cone
$(A,\alpha)$ over $F$ to be a feeble limit of $F$ if for any cone $(B,\beta)$ there
exists a strong epimorphism $p: E\to B$ and a morphism $f:E\to A$
	such that $f:(E,\beta p) \to (A,\alpha)$ is a cone morphism. This definition
agrees with the one given here as soon as the category is regular, and allows the
	extension of Lemmas \ref{feeble_is_pullback} and \ref{feeble_composite} (a) to
	arbitrary categories.
\end{remark}
\begin{lemma}\label{feeble_stable}
Let $\C$ be a finitely complete category. If pullbacks are stable under binary coproduct,
then feeble pullbacks are are also.
\end{lemma}
For a binary relation $(R,r_1,r_2)$ on $A$ in a category $\C$ we write
$(\bar R,\bar r_1,\bar r_2)$ for its reflexive closure. We have:
\begin{proposition}\label{proposition:7}
	Let $\C$ be a regular category satisfying Condition \ref{conditions}
       let $m:S\to A$ be a monomorphism and let $(R,r_1,r_2)$ be an equivalence
        relation on $S$ in $\C$. The reflexive closure of $(R,mr_1,mr_2)$ is
       an equivalence relation.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
	Note that the reflexive closure of $R$ can be constructed as the join of 
	$\langle mr_1,mr_2\rangle : R\to A\times A$ and $\langle 1_A,1_A\rangle: A\to A\times A$.
	Which can be constructed as the (regular epi, mono)-factorization of the 
	morphism $[\langle mr_1,mr_2\rangle,\langle 1_A,1_A\rangle] : R+A\to A\times A$
	as follows:
\[
	\xymatrix{
		R+A \ar[dr]_{[\langle mr_1,mr_2\rangle,\langle 1_A,1_A\rangle]\ \ }\ar[rr]^{e} && \bar R \ar[dl]^{\langle \bar r_1, \bar r_2\rangle}\\
		& A\times A.
	}
\]
	The relation $(\bar R, \bar r_1, \bar r_2)$ is easily shown to be symmetric
	and hence we only need to show that it is transitive.
Noting that $[\langle mr_1,mr_2\rangle,\langle 1_A,1_A\rangle] = \langle [mr_1,1_A],[mr_2,1_A] \rangle$
	we see that $\bar r_i e = [mr_i,1_A]$. Forming the pullback
\[
\xymatrix{
R\times_{\langle r_2,r_1\rangle}R\ar[d]_{\pi_1}\ar[r]^-{\pi_2} & R\ar[d]^{r_1}\\
R \ar[r]_{r_2} & S
}
\]
        we see that since each of the squares in the diagram
\[
\xymatrix@C=8ex{
R\times_{\langle r_2,r_1\rangle}R+R\ar[r]^-{\pi_2+r_2}\ar[d]_{\pi_1+1_R}&R +S \ar[r]^{1_R+m} \ar[d]_{r_1+1_S} & R+A\ar[d]^{r_1+1_A}\\
R+R \ar[r]^{r_2+r_2}\ar[d]_{[1_R,1_R]}&S+S \ar[r]^{1_S+m} \ar[d]_{[1_S,1_S]} & S+A\ar[d]^{[m,1_A]}\\
R\ar[r]_{r_2} &S \ar[r]^{m} & A
}
\]
are feeble pullbacks it follows by Lemma \ref{feeble_composite} that the outer morphisms form a feeble pullback too. Therefore the left hand
square in the diagram
	\[
		\xymatrix@C=12ex{
			(R\times_{\langle r_2,r_1\rangle}R+R)+(R+A) \ar[r]^-{(\pi_2+mr_2)+1_{R+A}} \ar[d]_{[\pi_1,1_R]+[mr_1,1_A]}&(R+A)+(R+A)\ar[r]^-{[1_{R+A},1_{R+A}]} \ar[d]_{[mr_1,1_A]+[mr_1,1_A]} & R+A\ar[d]^{[mr_1,1_A]}\\
			R+A\ar[r]_{mr_2+1_A}&A+A\ar[r]_{[1_A,1_A]} & A
		}
	\]
is by Lemma \ref{feeble_stable} a feeble pullback and hence by Lemma \ref{feeble_composite} so is the composite.
	This means that the diagram  
	\[
		\xymatrix@C=14ex{
			(R\times_{\langle r_2,r_1\rangle}R +R)+(R+A) \ar[r]^-{[\pi_2+mr_2,1_{R+A}]} \ar[d]_{[\pi_1,1_R]+[mr_1,1_A]} & R+A\ar[d]^{[mr_1,1_A]}\\
			R+A\ar[r]_{[mr_2,1_A]} & A
		}
	\]
	is a feeble pullback. Forming the pullback
\[
\xymatrix{
\overline R\times_{\langle\overline r_2,\overline r_1\rangle}\overline R\ar[d]_{\overline\pi_1}\ar[r]^-{\overline\pi_2} & \overline R\ar[d]^{\overline r_1}\\
\overline R \ar[r]_{\overline r_2} & A
}
\]
let us write $\tilde e$ for the unique morphism $ (R\times_{\langle r_2,r_1\rangle}R +R)+(R+A) \to \bar R\times_{\langle \bar r_2,\bar r_1\rangle} \bar R$ such that $\bar \pi_1 \tilde e  = e ([\pi_1,1_R]+[mr_1,1_A])$ and  $\bar \pi_2 \tilde e = e [\pi_2+mr_2,1_{R+A}]$. Let us write $\tau:R\times_{\langle r_2,r_1\rangle} R \to R$ for the unique morphism such that $r_1 \tau = r_1\pi_1$ and $r_2\tau = r_2\pi_2$ (which exists since $R$ is transitive). 
%Since each of the diagrams 
%	\[
%		\xymatrix{
%			& A\\
%			R\times_{\langle r_2,r_1\rangle}R
%			\ar[rr]^-{\iota_1\tau} 
%			\ar[dr]_{mr_1\pi_1}
