    \label{fig:graph_QCCC_N3}
\end{figure}

Following the above construction, we can similarly build the graphs that represent the SDP constraints for our other classes: these are shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:graph_QCconvFO_N3} for the \textup{\textsf{QC-convFO}} class (with $N=3$), in Fig.~\ref{fig:graph_QCQC} for the \textup{\textsf{QC-QC}} class (with $N=3$ and $4$), and in Fig.~\ref{fig:graph_QCsupFO_N3} for the \textup{\textsf{QC-supFO}} class (with $N=3$). These representations help visualise certain features of each class: e.g., that the \textup{\textsf{QC-convFO}} class involves independent orders, in parallel; that the graph of the \textup{\textsf{QC-QC}} class has an ``upside-down symmetry''; that the graph of the \textup{\textsf{QC-supFO}} class is the ``upside-down version'' of that of the \textup{\textsf{QC-CC}} class (reflecting the comment made a couple of paragraphs below Eq.~\eqref{eq:charact_W_QC_SupFO_decomp}).

