\section{Introduction}
%{\color{red}Conversational Recommender Systems (CRSs) have surfaced as a prominent research topic \cite{HyCoRec, shang2023multi, qian2023hutcrs}, with a focus on deciphering user preferences and dispensing recommendations through multi-turn dialogues between users and the system. But obstacles such as data imbalance and inconsistent user-item interactions easily precipitate unfair recommendation for disadvantaged users, who are then more susceptible to receiving unsatisfactory recommendations. Thus, alleviating fairness in the CRS is crucial to boost recommendation performance.}
Conversational Recommender Systems (CRSs) have become an important research area \cite{HyCoRec, HiCore, qian2023hutcrs}, focusing on understanding user preferences through multi-turn dialogues. However, issues like data imbalance and inconsistent user-item interactions can result in unfair recommendations for disadvantaged users, leading to unsatisfactory suggestions. Thus, it is rather crucial to alleviate the unfairness in the dynamic and interactive CRS.
%{\color{red}Recently, an increasing number of research efforts have focused on exploring fairness in Recommender Systems (RSs), which serve multiple-stakeholders including customers and providers. These fairness-aware recommendation studies can be classified into three categories. The first category \cite{customer_side_1,customer_side_2,customer_side_3,customer_side_4} emphasizes customer-side (\emph{i.e.}, user-side) fairness, aiming to ensure equitable treatment of users across groups (\emph{e.g.}, gender, race) to eliminate discrimination in recommendations.The second category \cite{provider_side_1,provider_side_2,provider_side_3,provider_side_4} focuses on provider-side (\emph{i.e.}, item-side) fairness, which guarantees equal recommendation opportunities for diverse item groups (\emph{e.g.}, popular vs. unpopular), fostering a fair platform for all providers. The third category \cite{two_sided_1,two_sided_2,two_sided_3,two_sided_4} explores two-sided fairness, striving to avoid bias in recommendations, maintaining balanced representation and equitable treatment for both users and items.}

Recently, there has been a growing interest in examining fairness in Recommender Systems (RSs), which cater to multiple stakeholders \cite{two_sided_3}, including customers (user side) and providers (item side). These fairness-aware recommendation methods can be categorized into three main areas. The first category \cite{customer_side_1,customer_side_2,customer_side_3,customer_side_4} focuses on customer-side fairness, aiming to ensure equitable treatment of users across different groups (\emph{e.g.}, gender, race) to eliminate discrimination in recommendations. The second category \cite{provider_side_1,provider_side_2,provider_side_3,provider_side_4} addresses provider-side (item-side) fairness, ensuring relatively fair recommendation opportunities for diverse item groups (\emph{e.g.}, popular vs. unpopular) to create a fair platform for all providers. The third category \cite{two_sided_1,two_sided_2,two_sided_3,two_sided_4} investigates two-sided fairness, which seeks to mitigate bias in recommendations by ensuring that both users and items are treated equitably. This highlights the significance of balanced representation.
%The dynamics of unfairness

{\color{black}
%Despite their effectiveness, most existing methods still suffer from two major issues. \emph{1) Unfairness Evolution.} Many methods focus on alleviating the unfairness in offline or nearly-static recommendation environments, often overlooking the inherently dynamic nature of unfairness in recommendations. In real-world scenarios, a feedback loop exists between users and the system, which can exacerbate unfairness over time as users interact with the system. This amplification of unfairness can lead to significant issues such as the Matthew effect \cite{MatthewEffect1}, filter bubbles \cite{filter_bubble}, and echo chambers \cite{echo_chamber}. Therefore, it is crucial to mitigate unfairness within dynamic and interactive CRSs. 
