**Response**: Thanks for your valuable comments! In our proposed method, the model complexity comprises four key tasks: **hypergraph construction**, **the convolution complexity**, **contrastive learning complexity**, and **line graph constructions**. We will provide the complexity discussion section in the final version.

1) The complexity of hypergraph construction is $O(n)$, where $n$ is the batch size. 

2) The convolution complexity is $O(n)$. 

3) The contrastive learning complexity—whether between hypergraphs, line graphs, or between each hypergraph and line graph—is also $O(n \times d)$, where $d$ is the feature dimension.

4) The original complexity of line graph construction is $O(n^2 \times e)$. However, we implement several strategies to optimize this complexity—including **Set Operation Optimization**, **JIT Compilation**, and **Loop Parallelization**—to reduce such complexity. Concretely, we applied the following optimization strategies: **a) Set Operation Optimization**: The set operations were replaced with numpy's intersect1d and union1d functions, reducing the complexity of calculating the Jaccard index from $O(e)$ to $O(k \log k)$, where $k$ is the number of hyperedges associated with each node. Particularly, in the case of Compressed Row Storage, where the hypergraph is sparse and typically $k \ll e$, the computational overhead is significantly reduced. **b) JIT Compilation**: We applied Just-In-Time (JIT) compilation using numba and njit, significantly improving the loop execution speed. Python code is compiled into efficient machine code, eliminating the performance limitations of the Python interpreter. **c) Parallelizing the Loop**: The double loop was parallelized using \texttt{prange}, allowing the loop to run on multiple CPU cores, further reducing the overall computation time. 

Therefore, the theoretical complexity of the optimized code is $O\left(\frac{n^2 k \log k}{p}\right)$, where $p$ is the number of CPU cores. Additionally, thanks to numba}'s JIT compilation, the optimized code is compiled into efficient machine code during execution, significantly enhancing performance compared to the original Python code and fully utilizing the hardware. Based on our experience, on multi-core machines (e.g., high-performance servers with 128 cores), the level of parallelization can reach $p \approx n$, and **the running speed can achieve near-linear acceleration**. In fact, on a server with two AMD EPYC 7502 processors and 128 logical CPUs, paired with an NVIDIA 3090 GPU, the training process takes less than a day. 
