\section{Numerical Experiments}

We now present a series of experiments in which we verify the proposed FEI scheme. We consider Sparse-view CT Imaging, which was also studied in~\cite{chen2021equivariant}. We show here that (i) FEI behaves well numerically in both performance and efficiency; (ii) it is better than Equivariant Imaging (EI); (iii) it performs even better when incorporating the PnP Prior; and (iv) PnP-FEI is the fastest unsupervised training algorithm.

Throughout the experiments, we compare our scheme (FEI) with (i) vanilla EI; (ii) PnP-FEI with a pre-trained denoiser, which in practice we consider DnCNN, and BM3D. For these denoising algorithms, we choose the standard deviation of the noise as $\sigma = 1.0 / \lambda$ ($\lambda$ is the Augemented Lagrangian parameter as presented in Algorithm~\ref{alg:PnP-LEI}) which are implemented via \texttt{DeepInv} toolbox\footnote{\url{https://deepinv.github.io/deepinv/}}. For a fair comparison with FEI, all the reported results for EI are obtained by directly running the released code of~\cite{chen2021equivariant}. We use the same U-Net to bulid $G_{\theta}$ as suggested in~\cite{chen2021equivariant} for EI, FEI and PnP-FEI. Specifically, $G_{\theta}$ is a four-depth residual U-Net~\citep{ronneberger2015u} with a symmetric encoder-decoder and long skip connections. Each encoder comprises two $3 \times 3$ Convolution-BatchNorm-ReLU blocks with identity shortcuts, followed by $2 \times 2$ max pooling to double channels $[64, 128, 256, 512]$. The decoder mirrors the encoder in a refinement manner, and a final $1 \times 1$ convolution restores the output. For the measurement consistency loss, we choose $\ell_2$ in this experiment for simplicity.

For the sparse-view CT task, when updating $\theta$, we use the Adam optimizer with an initial learning rate of 0.001 in all methods. The weight decay is $1 \times 10^{-8}$. We trained the networks for 10,000 epochs, keeping the learning rate constant for the first 5000 epochs, and then shrinking it by a factor of $0.5 \times (1+ \cos(\frac{\#\text{epoch}}{10000}\cdot \pi))$. We verify our model with equivariance strength $\alpha=100$ as suggested in~\cite{chen2021equivariant}. Other parameters of the FEI scheme are set as follows: Lagrangian multiplier $\lambda=1$; NAG momentum factor $\beta=0.1$; NAG step size $\eta=0.01$; NAG iterations $J=10$. The complete NAG algorithm is presented in Algorithm~\ref{alg:nag}. The gradient descent step size in PnP-FEI is $\gamma = 0.01$. We also use a moving average to smooth the reconstructed images for better reconstruction and smooth PSNR curves, the same as~\cite{sun2021plug}.

%\subsection{Sparse-view CT}


 The corresponding physics model $A$ of sparse-view CT is the \texttt{Radon} transformation where 50 scans (angles) are uniformly subsampled to generate sparse-view sinograms (observations) $y$. The transformation we consider here is a group of rotations by randomly rotating 1 degree over 360 degrees. We use 10 images sampled (\textit{index 1 to 10}) from the CT100~\citep{clark2013cancer} datatset to train, and use another 10 images (\textit{index 11 to 20}) with same number of scans to test the generalization performance of the networks trained by our methods. The image is resized to $128 \times 128$ (16384 pixels). 


 %The supervised trained residual U-Net is the FBPConvNet proposed in~\cite{jin2017deep} with the trained network available here\footnote{\url{https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1Io0quD-RvoVNkCmE36aQYpoouEAEP5pF?usp=sharing}} 

 %(\textit{note that the trained supervised model was train with CT100 dataset from index 1 to 90}). 

In order to better understand the boosts of proposed FEI scheme for sparse-view CT Imaging, we show the PSNR curves with respect to both iterations and times in Figure~\ref{fig:ct-psnr&mse-iters5000-curve}, and Table~\ref{tab: ct-psnr&mse-iters5000} shows the PSNR in the last iteration in Figure~\ref{fig:ct-psnr&mse-iters5000-curve}. In Figure~\ref{fig:ct-psnr&mse-iters5000-curve} we can observe that the PnP-FEI is able to provide order-of-magnitude ($\geq10\times$) acceleration over standard EI training.



 We then test our methods with 50 \texttt{Radon} scan number (same as training) on another 10 images (\textit{from index 11 to 20}), the results is reported in Table~\ref{tab: 50-ct-test}. We can observe that in terms of generalization performance, the networks trained by our FEI/PnP-FEI both provide better generalization compared to the network trained by standard EI.

