Following \cite{tsubota2024bifurcation}, we can then predict the leading-order spatial asymptotics of the front by transforming into a co-moving frame $z = x - \sigma(t)$, obtaining 
\begin{equation}\label{e:nu}
v(z,t) = \frac{1}{\sqrt{4\pi t}}\exp\left[-\frac{z^2}{4t} - \frac{\sigma(t)}{2t}z\right]
\end{equation}
so that the linear solution, and it turns out the nonlinear solution, has leading order spatial tail $u(z,t)\approx \re^{\nu(t) z}$ with $$\nu(t) = -\sigma(t)/(2t) = -\sqrt{\frac{\epsilon t}{2}} = -\sqrt{\mu(t)/2},$$ indicating that the front steepens as time increases. 

This accurately predicts the steepness of the front found in numerical simulation. Here we measure the time-dependent steepness by computing $u_x(x,t)/u(x,t)$ for $x$ values just ahead of the front interface.
