The families of \Cref{mainthm:nonzerofamilies} are counted in a maximal sense. For instance, \Cref{tab:classes} shows that $4\nu\kappabar$ generates a $v_2^{32}$-periodic family, which is actually stating that the known $v_2^{32}$-periodic family generated by $\kappabar$ supports multiplication by $4\nu$. In $\pi_{23}\Sph$ we have $\eta\eps\kappa = 4\nu\kappabar$. As both $\eps$ and $\kappa$ also generate $v_2^{32}$-periodic families, we count three $v_2^{32}$-periodic families generated by $4\nu\kappabar$. We do this to reflect the additional information that the periodic family generated by $\eps$ supports multiplication by $\eta\kappa$ and that the family generated by $\kappa$ supports multiplication by $\eta\eps$. This methodology is discussed in detail in the proof of \Cref{mainthm:nonzerofamilies} and summarised in \Cref{sssec:counting}.

%To reiterate, the elements in these periodic families lives in arbitrarily high degree within the stable stems an have Adams--Novikov filtration up to $24$
Geometric applications of this theorem are immediate. Kervaire--Milnor \cite{kervairemilnor} construct groups $\Theta_k$ of \emph{homotopy $k$-spheres}, which for $k\geq 5$ vanishes if and only if the $k$-sphere $S^k$ has a unique differentiable structure, courtesy of the $h$-cobordism theorem of Smale \cite{smale_hcobordism}. These groups are also related to $\pi_\ast\Sph$ via the isomorphism $\Theta_{4k} \simeq \pi_{4k} \Sph/\sim$ and the short exact sequence
\[0 \to \Theta_{2k+1}^\bp \to \Theta_{2k+1} \to \pi_{2k+1} \Sph/\sim \to 0,\]
where $\Theta_{2k+1}^\bp$ is the group of homotopy spheres which bound a paralellisable manifold, and $\pi_\ast\Sph/\sim$ is the quotient of the stable stems by the image of the $J$-homomorphism, a subgroup of the $v_1$-periodic families of Adams. As $v_2$-periodic families survive this quotient, the next result follows from \Cref{mainthm:nonzerofamilies}, building upon similar $v_2$-periodic families found in \cite[Cor.1.3]{exoticwithcokerJ}, \cite[Cor.1.5]{hurewicztmf}, and \cite{bbq,bq}.

\begin{coralph}\label{maincor:exotic}
    There are exotic spheres in all dimensions congruent to $72$, $144$, and $168$ modulo $192$. %\footnote{As mentioned in \cite{exoticwithcokerJ}, one can also make this conclusion concerning exotic spheres in degree $72$ from the nonvanishing of $\be_1^2\be_2^2$ at the prime $3$, so we are only contributing $v_2^{32}$-periodicity in this degree.} 
    There are very exotic spheres, meaning exotic spheres which do not bound a parallelisable manifold, in all dimensions congruent to $143$, $145$, and $169$ modulo $192$.
\end{coralph}

\subsection*{Proof of \Cref{mainthm:nonzerofamilies}}
Our proof of \Cref{mainthm:nonzerofamilies} proceeds in two main steps. First, we compute some of the image of the ANSS of $\Sph$ in the ANSS for $\TMF$, the latter of which is well-known, although still complicated; see \cite{smfcomputation} for more background. We formulate this with Pstragowski's category of \emph{$\MU$-synthetic spectra} \cite{syntheticspectra}, a convenient, and often necessary, homotopy theoretic category of spectral sequences. To determine families in this \emph{synthetic Hurewicz image} of $\TMF$, we combine information from the classical Hurewicz image \cite{hurewicztmf} of $\TMF$ with the use of \emph{modified ASSs}. In particular, we identify a number of families in the synthetic Hurewicz image of $\TMF$ not previously known; see \Cref{lm:47,lm:71,lm:95}. This gives us a huge collection of candidate classes in the ANSS of $\Sph$ whose behaviour in that of $\TMF$ we understand. As we are interested in families which vanish in $\pi_\ast\TMF$, this means that these candidate classes are all hit by differentials in the ANSS for $\TMF$.

Our second step, is to rule out the possibility that these differentials in the ANSS for $\TMF$ lift to differentials in the ANSS for $\Sph$. This would imply that these classes survive the ANSS for $\Sph$ and witness $v_2^{32}$-periodic families in $\pi_\ast\Sph$ at the prime $2$. Similar to Adams' use of Adams operations on $\KO$ to detect $v_1$-periodic families, we use operations on $\TMF$ to rule out the lifts of these differentials. More precisely, just as modular forms of level $N$ come with \emph{Atkin--Lehner involutions}, topological modular forms of level $N$ -- denoted as $\TMF_0(N)$ -- comes with a homotopical refinement of this involution $w\colon \TMF_0(N) \to \TMF_0(N)$, courtesy of the second-named author \cite{heckeontmf}. We then show the canonical map $p\colon \TMF \to \TMF_0(3)$ and its twist by $w$ equalises the target of these differentials, but not the source. This implies that the target lifts to the sphere, but the differential killing it does not. Most of the classes of \Cref{tab:classes} are then shown to be nonzero as they have nonzero image in this equaliser, denoted by $\J_0(3)$, reflected by a $\checkmark$ in the $\J_0(3)$-column; see \Cref{hurewiczforjzerothree}. Those remaining few classes are obtained with a careful Toda bracket argument; see \Cref{thm:todabracketdetection}.

These same techniques can also be used to recover Adams' $v_1$-periodic families, as demonstrated in \cite{syntheticj}. Our techniques suggest further avenues of research; see \Cref{ssec:questions}.

\subsection*{Related work}
As mentioned above, these are previous instances of $v_2^{32}$-periodic families not detected by $\TMF$ in the literature.

\begin{remark}
    In a preliminary report \cite[\textsection15]{tmfbook}, Hopkins--Mahowald mention many potential $v_2^{32}$-periodic families in $\pi_*\Sph_2$, some of which are outside the Hurewicz image of $\TMF$. This includes families generated by $\nu\kappabar$, $\nu^2\kappabar$, $[2\nu\kappabar\Delta]$, and $\eta[2\nu\kappabar\Delta]$ (see \cite[\textsection15: Pr.11.4-9]{tmfbook}). We prove the existence of such families in \Cref{mainthm:nonzerofamilies}, as can be read directly from \Cref{tab:classes} or \Cref{list}. In \cite[\textsection15:Pr.11.14-15]{tmfbook}, Hopkins--Mahowald also identify nonzero classes in $\pi_*\Sph_2$ detected in the synthetic homotopy groups of $\TMF$ by $[\nu\kappabar\Delta^2]$, $\eta^2[\nu\kappabar\Delta^2]$, and $\kappabar[\nu^2\Delta^2]$. Our \Cref{mainthm:nonzerofamilies} recovers these results and shows that these classes also generate $v_2^{32}$-periodic families.
\end{remark}

\begin{remark}
    Both families in \Cref{tab:classes} generated by the class $\kappa\kappabar^2$ using the periodicities of $\kappa$ and $\kappabar$ appear in \cite[Tab.1]{exoticwithcokerJ}. The family in degree $120$ generated by $\kappabar^6$ also appears in \cite[Pr.12.1]{exoticwithcokerJ}.
\end{remark}

\begin{remark}\label{rmk:bbq}
    In \cite{bbq}, Bhattacharya--Bobkova--Quigley use the type 2 complex $A_1$ to produce $2$-torsion $v_2^{32}$-periodic families in the congruence classes $23$, $47$, $71$, $74$, $95$, $119$, and $167$. In \cite{bq}, Bobkova--Quigley exhaust this technique to produce $\eta$-torsion $v_2^{32}$-periodic families in the congruence classes $73$ and $120$. Whereas our families are produced explicitly from a generator in $\pi_*\Sph$ and a self-map, the methods of these authors show the existence of a family without identifying it explicitly. Nonetheless, Bhattacharya--Bobkova--Quigley speculate in \cite[Rmk.1.1]{bbq} on the generators of each of their families. If these speculations hold, then each of their families contains a common subfamily with one of the families of \Cref{mainthm:nonzerofamilies} by \Cref{cortoinfiniteagreement}. Similarly, we speculate that each of the Bobkova--Quigley families contains a common subfamily with one of our families in congruence classes $73$ and $120$.
\end{remark}

\Cref{mainthm:nonzerofamilies} refines all of the above works by first identifying these nonzero $v_2^{32}$-periodic families in the synthetic Hurewicz image of $\TMF$. This systematic approach then allows us to easily detect these families in the sphere using operations on $\TMF$.

