\section{Detecting new $v_2^{32}$-periodic families in stable stems}\label{sec:periodicfamilies}
Equipped with an adequate knowledge of the synthetic Hurewicz image of $\TMF_\BP$, we can focus our attention on proving \Cref{mainthm:nonzerofamilies}. This is cut into two pieces: arguments using Toda brackets and arguments using the Hurewicz image of $\J_0(3)$.

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\subsection{Forcing an empty Toda bracket}\label{sec:todabracket}
There are a collection of classes which we know to be $\tau$-power torsion and some nonzero $\tau$-multiple lies in the synthetic Hurewicz image of $\TMF_\BP$ by \Cref{synthetichurewiczintext}. We will see that many of these classes are $\tau$-torsion free in $\Sph_\BP$ using $\J_0(3)$, but for a small collection of examples, we require the following alternative argument by ``forcing empty Toda brackets''.

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:todabracketdetection}
    Using the lifts of $\kappabar$ and $[\nu^2\Delta^2]$ to top-cells of type $2$-complexes found in \cite[Lm.7.17(2)]{hurewicztmf} and \Cref{lm:54}, the corresponding $v_2^{32}$-periodic families generated by the products $\nu^2\kappabar\in \pi_{26} \Sph$ and $\kappabar[\nu^2\Delta^2], \kappa\kappabar^3 \in \pi_{74} \Sph$ are all nonzero in $\pi_\ast \Sph$ of order $2$ and $4$, respectively. In particular, these products generate four $v_2^{32}$-periodic families in total.
\end{theorem}

Many other nonzero families from \Cref{tab:classes} can also be obtained directly using the following argument. One can also adapt this argument to show that $\eta^3\neq 0$ using the HFPSS for $\KO$; we leave these details to the interested reader.

\begin{proof}
    First, consider the family $\{\nu^2\kappabar_r\}_r$, where $\{\kappabar_r\}$ is the $v_2^{32}$-periodic family generated by $\kappabar$ à la \cite[Lm.7.17(2)]{hurewicztmf}. We claim that each element $\nu^2 \kappabar_r \in \pi_{26+192r} \Sph$ is nonzero. To see this, first note that $\nu^2 \kappabar_r$ lifts to a class $\pi_{26+192r,6} \Sph_\BP$ which maps to the nonzero class $\nu^2\kappabar\Delta^{8r}$ in $\TMF_\BP$ by \Cref{synthetichurewiczintext}. Consider the Toda bracket $\angbr{\kappabar_r, \tau^4\nu^2, 2} \subseteq \pi_{27+192r,1}\TMF_\BP$. The indeterminacy of this bracket is $2\pi_{27+192r,1} \TMF_\BP$. By an $E_5$-page Massey product computation and the synthetic Moss' theorem \cite[Th.3.16]{smfcomputation}, we see that this bracket contains the generator $[2\nu\Delta^{1+8r}]$, hence this bracket in $\TMF_\BP$ does not contain zero. However, the group $\pi_{27+192r,1}\TMF_\BP$ is $\tau$-torsion free, and by \cite[Th.1.2]{hurewicztmf} the Hurewicz image of $\TMF$ in $\pi_{27}\TMF$ is zero, hence the synthetic Hurewicz image of $\TMF_\BP$ in this degree is also zero. The functorality of Toda brackets dictates that the bracket $\angbr{\kappabar_r, \tau^4 \nu^2, 2}$ defined in $\Sph_\BP$ has to map to the same bracket in $\TMF_\BP$, which from the above considerations means this bracket in $\Sph_\BP$ must be empty. By \cite[Pr.3.11(1)]{smfcomputation}, the bracket above is empty if and only if either $2\tau^4\nu^2 \neq0$ or $\tau^4 \nu^2\kappabar_r \neq 0$. The former is nonsense as $2\nu^2=0$, so we see that $\tau^4\nu^2\kappabar_r\neq0$, meaning $\nu^2\kappabar_r$ cannot be a $d_5$-boundary. Moreover, this class cannot by a $d_r$-boundary for $r\geq 6$ for degree reasons. In particular, $\nu^2\kappabar_r$ is $\tau$-torsion free and hence $\tau$-inverts to a nonzero class in $\pi_{26+192r} \Sph$, as desired.

    For $\kappabar[\nu^2\Delta^2]$, we use the bracket $\angbr{\kappabar, [\nu^2\Delta^2], 4}$. Using the $v_2^{32}$-periodicity of $\kappabar$ gives one family and the $v_2^{32}$-periodicity of $[\nu^2\Delta^2]$, which can be chosen to be $4$-torsion by \Cref{lm:54}, produces another. For $\kappa\kappabar^3$, we use the bracket $\angbr{\kappabar^3,\kappa,2}$.
\end{proof}

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\subsection{On the Hurewicz image of height $2$ image-of-$J$ spectra}\label{sec:hurewiczofjzerothree}
Combining our knowledge of the synthetic Hurewicz image of $\TMF_\BP$ together with the ``deleting differentials''-technique, we immediately obtain some elements in the classical Hurewicz image of $\J_0(3)$.

\begin{theorem}\label{hurewiczforjzerothree}
    All $v_2^{32}$-periodic families of \Cref{tab:classes} with a $\checkmark$ in the $\J_0(3)$-column have nonzero image in $\J_0(3)$.
\end{theorem}

There are many other classes in the Hurewicz image on $\J_0(3)$ in the image of the boundary map $\pi_{\ast+1} \TMF_0(3) \to \pi_\ast \J_0(3)$. These include all of the divided $\al$-family and any $v_2$-periodic families will be explored in future work together with the Hurewicz image of $\Q(3)$.

\begin{proof}
    Consider \Cref{cor:synthetichurewiczimpractice} with
    \[R=\Sph_\BP, \qquad A=\TMF_\BP, \qquad B=\TMF_0(3), \qquad \varphi = q, \qquad \psi = p\]
    and $x=\nu\kappabar$. In this case, we have $d_5$-differentials $d_5(\pm(4k+1)\Delta) = \nu\kappabar$ for all integers $k$ by \cite[Pr.6.19]{smfcomputation}. These are all possible differentials witnessing $\nu\kappabar$ as a $d_5$-boundary, up to a sign. Lifting these elements to $\TMF_\BP/\tau^4$ as in \cite[Not.6.18]{smfcomputation}, we see that $(q-p)(\Delta)$ is nonzero courtesy of \Cref{pr:injectivityofpandq}. In particular, $\nu\kappabar$ is not a $d_5$-boundary in $\sigma(\J_0(3)_\BP)$ by \Cref{cor:synthetichurewiczimpractice}. The class $\nu\kappabar$ lives in $\pi_{23,5}\J_0(3)_\BP$ and $\pi_{n,f}\J_0(3)_\BP/\tau$ vanishes for $f\leq -1$ by \Cref{lm:nothingbelowzero}, so $\tau$-inverts to a nonzero class of the same name in the Hurewicz image of $\J_0(3)$ by \Cref{cor:synthetichurewiczimpractice}. Moreover, the $2$-extension $4\nu = \tau^2 \eta^3$, see \cite[Lm.6.16]{smfcomputation}, shows that $\eta^3\kappabar$ lies in the Hurewicz image of $\J_0(3)$ too, and that $\nu\kappabar$ has order $8$. This argument works equally as well for all elements in the $v_2^{32}$-periodic family generated by $\nu\kappabar$.

