Bold lettering refers to which elements act as the periodicity generator, the existence of such classes with prescribed periodicity being justified by the proofs of \Cref{thm:todabracketdetection} or \Cref{mainthm:nonzerofamilies} above. Square brackets indicate indecomposable elements in $\pi_\ast\Sph$. Elements are named by their image in the ANSS for $\TMF$. All families are $2$-torsion except for those in $23a$ and $71a$ which are $8$-torsion, and in $47a$, $74a$, and $119a$ which are $4$-torsion.

One then counts the number of $v_2^{32}$-periodic families roughly by counting each bold factor, with the following caveats: in the $48$-stem, the families $\kappa^2\kappabar$ and $\eps\kappabar^2$ generated by the $v_2^{32}$-periodicity of $\kappabar$ are the same, as in $\pi_{28}\Sph$ we have $\kappa^2 = \eps\kappabar$ by \cite[Th.11.61(28)]{brunerrognes}. Similarily, in the $145$-stem, the families $\eta\kappa\kappabar[\kappa\Delta^4]$ and $\nu[\eps\Delta][\kappa\Delta^4]$ generated by the $v_2^{32}$-periodicity of $[\kappa\Delta^4]$ are the same, as $\nu[\eps\Delta] = \eta\kappa\kappabar$ in $\pi_{35} \Sph$ by \cite[Th.11.61(35)]{brunerrognes}. Further knowledge of the product structure of $\pi_\ast\Sph$ could identify more families.
%One could also try to lift classes to various different type $2$-complexes with various different $v_2^{32k}$-self maps an hence produce even more potential families whenever from \Cref{tab:classes}. Of course, all of these potentially new families would have infinite intersection with our families of \Cref{tab:classes} by \Cref{mainthm:infiniteagreement}.\\

