\section{Introduction}
\label{sec-1}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
Periodically driven quantum systems are known to induce interesting phases that are otherwise not present in the system in equilibrium. One of the most remarkable examples of this is the unconventional superconductivity induced by exciting samples with lasers in the non-superconducting phase of several cuprates ~\cite{light_sc_cuprates,light_cuprates2} and more recently in fullerene~\cite{fulleren_sc}. There is a plethora of other exciting physics that emerges in systems under periodic drive, e.g., light-induced anomalous Hall effect in monolayer graphene~\cite{graphene_AQH} and on the 2D surface of a 3D topological insulator~\cite{AQHE}, light-induced many-body localization~\cite{MBL_light}, creation of long-range order like charge-density-wave and spin-density wave order~\cite{AFMorder,charge_order}, or even melting of the existing long-range order~\cite{melting} is possible by exciting samples with high-frequency light. Periodically driven quantum systems have been shown
to host novel non-equilibrium phases of matter like discrete-time crystals that
are impossible to achieve in equilibrium systems~\cite{DTC1, DTC2, DTC3}. Periodically driven cold atomic systems have recently
engineered models otherwise inaccessible in static systems, e.g., drive-engineered gauge fields~\cite{gauge}, Haldane model~\cite{Haldane}, and other topological systems~\cite{Harper, Hofstadter}. In this work, we propose a mechanism to realize an antiferromagnetic half-metal using a periodic drive starting from a weakly correlated metal or an insulator.  

Generic periodically driven systems are expected to absorb energy indefinitely and reach a locally infinite-temperature-like state~\cite{LDM_PRE, Alessio2014} when the drive parameters are of the same order as that of the leading couplings of the undriven part of the Hamiltonian. However, two different regimes of stability are known to emerge when (a) the drive frequency $\Omega$ is large and (b) when the drive amplitude is large compared to the leading parameters of the static part of the Hamiltonian. In case (a), one observes a long prethermal stability: For sufficiently large driving frequency $\Omega$, that is, $\Omega\gg W$ where $W$ is the local bandwidth of the system, these heating times will be exponentially large $t_{th} \ge O(\exp{\Omega/W})$~\cite{Dima_Pretherm_PRL, Mori_Kuwahara_Saito_Prethermal_PRL, Kuwahara_Mori_Saito, Dima_Floquet_Prethermalization,
Prethermal_Without_T, Mori_2022, Ho_Mori_Abanin_Pretherm_Rev}.
In the other case, (b),  when the drive field is strong enough (typically larger than some system-dependent threshold value), 
a new scenario known as dynamical freezing (DF) emerges.
In the DF regime, new approximate but stable conservation laws emerge that arrest thermalization and stabilize the system to non-trivial states with controlled entanglement entropy~\cite{AD-DMF, AD-SDG, Mahesh_Freezing, Naveen_Dynamical_Freezing, Onset, Asmi_DF_PRX_2021, Asmi_Flq_Rev, Debanjan_FlowEq_DF, Debanjan_Frozonium_DF, Adhip_Diptiman, Diptiman_DF, Bhaskar_DF, Somsubhra_Paul_Krishnendu_DF}. Recently, the stability has been shown in the thermodynamic limit by direct simulation of infinite systems~\cite{DF_TDL_iTEBD}. The stability of clean, interacting Floquet matter, covering and far surpassing any experimentally accessible regime, is hence well-established now. Thus, working with high frequency and high drive amplitude provides an alternative route toward
engineering rich, dynamically stabilized systems hosting novel phases of matter not easily realizable in an equilibrium setting. 


%\cite{Mori_2022}.
%T the realization of nonequilibrium phases by delaying the "heat-death" %being in a long-lived “prethermal” regime where the system can exhibit 

In this work, we focus on the periodically driven weakly interacting Hubbard model. We show that a sublattice-dependent driving field can transform a weakly interacting metal or an insulator into a half-metallic phase in which electrons with one spin polarization are conducting while the electrons with the opposite spin polarization are insulating. Basically, the periodic drive converts a weakly interacting Hubbard model with nearest-neighbor hopping into an intermediate to strongly interacting system which has a staggered potential $\Delta$, staggered second and third neighbor hopping, and correlated hopping on nearest-neighbour bonds. Although this effective model has similarity to the ionic Hubbard model (IHM)~\cite{IHM1,IHM2,cold_atom_IHM}, it is actually different in many interesting ways from the standard IHM. One can intuitively understand the formation of a half-metal in the driven system as follows. A large staggered potential supports different environments for up and down spins, with up spins mainly residing on, say, the A sublattice and down spins mainly residing on the B sublattice. This also makes the single-particle excitation gaps for the up- and down-spin channels different in the half-filled system. A doped system can then easily achieve a half-metallic phase with a shift of the Fermi level inside the valence band of only one spin component. We believe that this non-equilibrium mechanism for achieving a half-metal phase can be realized in cold atom experiments where the Hubbard model has been simulated~\cite{cold_atom_Hubbard}. 
\\

%Though drive-induced bare staggered potential and higher-range hoppings are not very large always, close to the dynamical freezing point, where the renormalized nearest-neighbour hopping of the driven system is highly suppressed, the system enters into strongly correlated regime with large staggered potential and sufficiently large staggered hoppings.
\begin{center}
    \textbf{Periodically Driven Hubbard Model and its Floquet Phases} \\
\end{center}
The focus of this work is to realize a stable half-metallic phase
employing the idea of dynamical freezing~\cite{AD-DMF,Onset,Asmi_DF_PRX_2021} in the Fermi-Hubbard model on a bipartite lattice, simply by driving the site energies in a staggered way. The model is
described by the following time-dependent Hamiltonian.     
\[H(t) =H_{Hub}+H_{drive}(t)\] 
\begin{equation}
\begin{split}
 H_{Hub}=-t_0\sum_{\langle ij\rangle,\sigma}c^\dagger_{iA\sigma}c_{jB\sigma}+ h.c.+U\sum_{i}n_{i\uparrow}n_{i\downarrow}-\mu\sum_i n_i 
 \\
 H_{drive}(t)=\frac{V}{2}sin(\Omega t-\Phi)\sum_{j\in A}\hat{n}_{j}+\frac{V}{2}sin(\Omega t+\Phi)\sum_{j\in B}\hat{n}_{j}
\end{split}
\label{hamil}
\end{equation}
Here, $t_0$ is the nearest neighbor hopping (indicated by $<ij>$), $U$ is the onsite repulsion between electrons, and $\mu$ is the chemical potential that tunes the average density in the system. $H_{drive}(t)$ describes a time-periodic single particle Hamiltonian with driving frequency $\Omega$ and amplitude $V$ which are the same for both sublattices, and a phase difference $2\Phi$ between two sublattices. 

The time dependence in the above Hamiltonian can be transformed into an effective time-dependent hopping by going to a rotating frame "by setting'' $c^\dagger_{i\sigma} \rightarrow \exp(-iF_i(t))c^\dagger_{i\sigma}$ where $F_i(t)=\frac{V}{2}\int_0^t \sin(\Omega t \mp \Phi)$ for $i\in A(B)$ respectively~\cite{Polkovnikov}.  The kinetic energy in the rotating frame is
\begin{equation}
H_{hopp}(t)=-t_0g(t)\sum_{\langle ij\rangle,\sigma}c^\dagger_{iA\sigma}c_{jB\sigma}+ h.c. 
\end{equation}

with $g(t)=\exp(i\frac{V}{\Omega}\sin(\Omega t)\sin(\Phi))$. The other terms in the Hamiltonian $H_{hub}$ remain unchanged under rotation. Using the Jacobi-Anger identity, one can write the time-dependent hopping term in the form of a series as shown below:
\begin{equation}
H_{hopp}(t)=-t_0\sum_{l=-\infty}^{\infty} \mathcal{J}_l(\frac{V}{\Omega}\sin(\Phi)) e^{il\Omega t} \sum_{\langle ij\rangle,\sigma}c^\dagger_{i\sigma}c_{j\sigma}+ h.c.
\end{equation}
Here, $\mathcal{J}_l(\frac{V}{\Omega}\sin(\Phi))$ is the $l^{th}$ Bessel function of the first kind. The leading corrections to the stroboscopic Floquet Hamiltonian resulting from this periodic driving are then found from the Magnus expansion~\cite{Polkovnikov}. We will keep terms up to order $1/\Omega$ in the Magnus expansion, which results in the following Floquet Hamiltonian $H_F=H_F^{[0]}+H_F^{[1]}$ with (for details see Supplemental Material (SM)~\cite{SM})
\begin{widetext}
\begin{equation}
\begin{split}
    H_F^{[0]} =-t_{eff}\sum_{\langle ij \rangle, \sigma} c_{i,\sigma}^\dagger c_{j,\sigma} + h.c + U \sum_j n_{j \uparrow} n_{j \downarrow} -\mu\sum_i n_i, \\
    H_F^{[1]}=\Delta_{ind}\sum_{\langle ij\rangle,\sigma}  \big( n_{i \sigma}^A - n_{j, \sigma}^B \big)+2t_{ind}^\prime\sum_{(ij),\sigma}\big( c_{iA,\sigma}^\dagger c_{jA,\sigma}
- c_{iB, \sigma}^\dagger c_{jB, \sigma} \big)  +h.c.\\
+t^{\prime}_{ind} \sum_{((ij)),\sigma}\big( c_{iA,\sigma}^\dagger c_{jA,\sigma}
- c_{iB, \sigma}^\dagger c_{jB, \sigma} \big)  +t_{ind}^{c}\sum_\sigma \big(n_{i \sigma}^A -n_{j, \sigma}^B \big)c_{iA \bar{\sigma}}^\dagger c_{jB, \bar{\sigma}} + h.c.
\end{split}    
\label{HF1}
\end{equation}
\end{widetext}
Here, $t_{eff} = t_0 \mathcal{J}_0(\frac{V}{\Omega}sin(\Phi))$ where $\mathcal{J}_0$ is the zeroth-order Bessel function of the first kind. For the range of parameters where $\mathcal{J}_0$ hits zero, the effective hopping of nearest neighbors is suppressed, resulting in the well-known dynamical freezing of the system\cite{AD-DMF,Onset,Asmi_DF_PRX_2021} . Even more interestingly, the drive induces a staggered potential $\Delta_{ind}$ between sublattices A and B that breaks the translational symmetry of the original lattice. This folds the Brillouin zone, resulting in a spectral gap in the single-particle excitation spectrum and a density difference between the two sublattices. The drive also induces second and third neighbor hoppings (indicated by $(ij)$ and $((ij))$, of strength $2t^\prime_{ind}$ and $t^\prime_{ind}$ respectively, providing dynamics within each sublattice. Note that higher-range hoppings induced by the drive are different in nature from conventional higher-range hopping terms. The drive-induced hopping amplitude is staggered on the two sublattices and does not break particle-hole symmetry, unlike the conventional second-neighbor hopping term. The drive also induces a correlated hopping term $t^c_{ind}$ that provides spin-dependent nearest-neighbor hopping and effectively prohibits the hopping of a doublon or a holon on the nearest-neighbor bond. Detailed expressions for $\Delta_{ind}$, $t^\prime_{ind}$, and $t^{c}_{ind}$ are provided in the SM~\cite{SM}. 

In the non-interacting limit, $U=0$, the dispersion of $H_F/t_{eff}$ is given by $E_{1,2}(k)= -\mu_{eff} \pm \sqrt{(\epsilon^\prime(k)+\Delta_{eff})^2 +\epsilon_0(k)^2}$. Here, $\epsilon_0(k)=2[cosk_x+cosk_y]$ and $\epsilon^\prime(k) = -8t^\prime_{eff} cosk_x cosk_y -2t^\prime_{eff}[cos(2k_x)+cos(2k_y)]$ with $t^\prime_{eff}=t^\prime_{ind}/t_{eff}$,$\mu_{eff}=\mu/t_{eff}$ and $\Delta_{eff}=\Delta_{ind}/t_{eff}$. Starting with a half-filled system that is metallic with $\mu=0$, the drive induces a spectral gap in the single-particle excitation spectrum, $E_{gap} = 2|\Delta_{eff}+4t^\prime_{eff}|$.  Thus, the staggered 2nd and 3rd neighbor hoppings add to the spectral gap along with the staggered potential, unlike a conventional second-order hopping, which can suppress the gap induced by the staggered potential. Before the analysis of the interacting system, we first disucss how various couplings in the Hamiltonian of Eqn.[4] evolve as a function of the drive.
