\\

\noindent
{\bf{Effective Couplings in the Driven Hubbard Model :}}
In this work, although we set the drive parameters 
slightly away from the dynamical freezing peak, the system will still have enough suppression of $t_{eff}$. {\it{This won't compromise the stability of the system -- it has been shown that Dynamical freezing is robust away from the freezing peaks when the drive is strong enough, albeit with increased fluctuations compared to that at a freezing peak ~\cite{DF_TDL_iTEBD}. However, the fluctuations can be tuned down arbitrarily in a controlled manner by ramping up the drive strength~\cite{Onset}.} }
The suppression in $t_{eff}$ effectively increases the e-e interactions significantly along with a sufficiently large enhancement of higher range hoppings and the staggered potential in the system, which helps to achieve a ferrimagnetic half-metal phase. This is depicted in Fig.~\ref{couplings}, which presents various couplings in $H_F$ as a function of the phase $\Phi$ for fixed values of $U$, the drive-amplitude $V$ and the drive-frequency $\Omega$. As $\Phi$ is tuned from $0$ to $\pi/2$, the nearest neighbour hopping $t_{eff}$ reduces reaching its minimum at $\Phi=\pi/2$. For $ \pi/2 < \Phi < \pi$, $t_{eff}(\Phi)=t_{eff}(\pi-\Phi)$. Panel (b) in Fig.\ref{couplings} shows the coefficient of the drive-induced second and third-neighbor hoppings in the units of $t_{eff}$, that is, $t^\prime_{eff}=t^\prime_{ind}/t_{eff}$. $|t^\prime_{eff}|$ increases as $\Phi$ increases from $0$ to $\pi/2$. Note that the drive-induced staggered potential $\Delta_{eff} = \Delta_{ind}/t_{eff}$ is equal to the higher-range hopping $t^\prime_{eff}$. Starting with an initial e-e interaction of $U=1.0$, the effective interaction in the driven system is also enhanced as shown in the plot of $U_{eff}=U/t_{eff}$ in panel (c). The correlated hopping depends not only on the drive parameters but also on the initial value of $U$. For $U=1.0$, $t_{corr}=t^c_{ind}/t_{eff}$ increases as $\Phi$ increases becoming maximum at $\pi/2$. In short, starting from a simple weakly interacting system with $U=1.0$ and $t_0=1$, one ends up with a complex strongly interacting system which, to some extent, looks similar to IHM explored in several earlier works~\cite{IHM_AG1,IHM_Craco,IHM_Kampf,IHM_AG2,IHM_Hoang,IHM_Bag}. But $H_F$ has interesting differences from the IHM in terms of the staggered higher-range hoppings and the spin-dependent correlated hopping. \\


