\hskip-0.02in
\includegraphics[width=1.0\columnwidth,height=2.6in]{phase_diag_new.pdf}
\caption{Panel (a): Maximum of the valence band for the down-spin, $E_{2\downarrow}(k)$, as a function of $\Phi$ for various values of particle densities. Panel (b): Phase diagram in the $\Phi-n$ plane for the drive with $V=60$ and $\Omega=30$. }
\label{pd}
\end{figure}

We have also calculated the single-particle spin- and sublattice- resolved density of states (DOS)(in units of $t_{eff}^{-1}$) $\rho_{\alpha,\sigma}(\omega) = -\frac{1}{\pi}\sum_k Im [G_{\alpha,\sigma}(k,\omega^{+})]$. $\rho_\sigma(\omega)$ shown in Fig.~\ref{dos} is the sum of two sublattice density of states, i.e.$\rho_{A\sigma}(\omega)+\rho_{B\sigma}(\omega)$. Fig.~\ref{dos} shows the DOS for both the spin bands as functions of $\omega$ for two densities and two values of $\Phi$ for each $n$. For $\Phi < \Phi_c$, where the system has spin degeneracy, $\rho_{\uparrow}(\omega)=\rho_{\downarrow}(\omega)$ with both $\rho_\uparrow(\omega=0)$ and $\rho_\downarrow(\omega=0)$ being finite. This is the paramagnetic metallic phase shown in panels (c) and (d) of Fig.\ref{dos} for two different densities. For $\Phi> \Phi_c$, as in panels (a) and (b) of Fig.~\ref{dos}, the Fermi level falls inside the valence band only for the up-spin; thus $\rho_{\uparrow}(\omega=0)$ is nonzero, while $\rho_{\downarrow}(\omega=0) =0$. This is the drive-induced ferrimagnetic half-metallic phase for which the up-spin band is conducting and the down-spin band is insulating.  

To compare the onset of the half-metal phase for systems with various densities, we carried out the above analysis for a range of densities. For all the densities studied, the conduction band is above the Fermi level for both spins, whereas the top of the down-spin valence band moves below the Fermi-level for $\Phi> \Phi_c$. As shown in Fig.~\ref{pd}, $\Phi_c$ increases with decreasing $n$, resulting in a wider HM phase for systems with larger densities (i.e. closer to one). This is because, as $n$ reduces, a larger $U_{eff}$ is required to turn on the magnetic order, which happens at a larger $\Phi_c$.  For any $n <1$, the HM phase exists for a range of $\Phi$ above $\Phi_c$ and is bounded from above by $\Phi=\pi/2$. \\

\noindent
{\bf{Half-filled system:}}
The half-filled system is special and shows a very different phase diagram, with a reentrant phase transition. The initial system with $U=1.0$ is an antiferromagnetic insulator. As $\Phi$ increases beyond $\Phi_1\approx 0.1$, the AFM order is lost due to the enhanced $\Delta_{eff}$ and $t_{eff}$ transforming the system into a paramagnetic band-insulator. Upon further increasing $\Phi$, the spin symmetry is broken again at $\Phi_2 \approx 0.86$ and the system becomes an AFM Mott insulator for all higher values of $\Phi$ up to $\pi/2$. In a very narrow regime close to $|\Phi-\Phi_1|\le 0.05$ and $\Phi\sim\Phi_2$ the top of the valence band for the up-spin channel almost touches the chemical potential with $|E_{2\uparrow}^{max}|\le 0.003$. Such a system can easily become half-metal in the presence of a tiny Zeeman field. Despite having enough higher-range hoppings, the system is unable to stabilize a half-metal phase at half-filling because these hoppings are staggered and do not disrupt the particle-hole symmetry. This is in complete contrast to the occurrence of a broad half-metal phase at half-filling in conventional IHM with the second neighbor hopping~\cite{Bag}.  We would like to emphasize that the half-filled system has different spectral gaps in the up and down spin channels for $\Phi> \Phi_2$, which makes it possible to turn it into a half-metal when the density is less than one, as we have shown. The detailed results for the half-filled case are presented in the SM~\cite{SM}. \\

%As $\Phi$ increases, first the staggered potential and the higher range hoppings increase faster than $U_{eff}$. At certain $\Phi$, e-e interactions become ineffective in comaprison the staggered potential and the large spectral-gap induced by higher-range hoppings, that the magnetization is killed and the system becomes a paramganetic correlated band-insulator.  For a range of $\Phi$ the system remains in the correlated band-insulator (CBI) phase. Only when $U_{eff}$ can overcome the gap the induced by the higher range hoppings and staggered potential, that the magnetization turns on again. This is an AFM Mott-insulating phase where the spectral gap keeps increasing monotonically with  $U_{eff}$. 
