\noindent
{\bf{$U$-dependence of the Half-Metal Phase:}}
So far, we have presented results for the system with the value of the onsite interaction fixed at $U=1.0$ for various densities. To explore the effect of $U$ on the width of the drive-induced half-metal phase, we also analyzed the system for various values of $U$ for $n=0.95$. As $U$ increases, the magnetization turns on at smaller values of $\Phi$, thereby increasing the width of the half-metal phase. %For $\Phi < \Phi_c$, which depends on initial U, bands $E_{1(2)\sigma}(k)$  do not depend on the initial interaction strength. Thus, for all values of initial $U$, for small $\Phi$ maximum of $E_{2\sigma}$ shown in Fig.~\ref{diffU} is the same.  
Due to the enhanced values of $m_s$ for larger values of $U$, the difference between the maximum value of $E_{2\uparrow}(k)$ and $E_{2\downarrow}(k)$ is also greater. This makes the half-metal phase more robust in systems with larger initial values of $U$. \\
