\noindent
{{\bf Alternate Driving Protocol:}}
We also studied the system in an alternate driving protocol in which the phase $\Phi$ is kept fixed and the amplitude and frequency of the drive are tuned. Here, $V/\Omega$ and $\Phi$ were kept fixed at $2.5$ and $\pi/4$ respectivel, resulting in a sufficient suppression of $t_{eff} (=0.3589)$ while keeping the system away from the dynamical freezing point. As $V$ in increased, the properties of the system are governed mainly by the drive induced higher range hoppings and the staggered potential because $U_{eff}=U/t_{eff}$ in this protocol is fixed. Since $|t^\prime_{eff}|$ as well as $|\Delta_{eff}|$ is large at smaller values of $V$, the staggered magnetization increases while the density difference between the two sublattices decreases as $V$ increases. $m_f$ stays fixed at $n-1$ for all $V$ values inside the half-metal phase. Interestingly, in this protocol the system hosts a ferrimagnetic half-metallic phase for a very wide range of parameters as shown in Fig~\ref{phipi4}.     \\

\begin{center}
   {\bf{Conclusions and Outlook}} 
\end{center}

In conclusion, we have studied the periodically driven Hubbard model on a bipartite lattice in which the drive induced staggered potential and staggered higher-order hoppings, along with reduced first neighbor hopping, effectvely equivalent to enhanced e-e interactions, give rise to a ferrimagnetic half-metallic phase. We studied the system under the driving protocol when the drive amplitude and the frequency are fixed at values significantly higher than any energy scale in the system. As we turn on the drive in a weakly interacting hole-doped Hubbard model, a ferrimagnetic order sets in via a first-order transition at some threshold value of the staggered phase $\Phi$ of the drive. The width of the half-metal phase along $\Phi$ is largest for systems with larger densities as long as it is below half-filling. As $\Phi$ increases the half-filled system shows a reentrant transition from AFM insulator to paramagnetic band insulator to AFM Mott insulator. Both the AFM insulators have different spectral gaps for the up and the down spin bands. %For a fixed density, the width of the HM phase is larger for systems with larger values of the e-e interaction. We the drive-induced mechanism for half-metallically described in our paper is novel. 
Though we have done the analysis by keeping only the zeroth-order and first-order terms in the Floquet Hamiltonian, for large drive frequency, we expect the contributions of higher-order terms in the Floquet Hamiltonian to be very small.  For high drive frequency, i.e., in the prethermal regime, it should be possible to observe the proposed half-metal phase for exponentially large time in the drive amplitude and frequency. For large drive amplitude, i.e., in the Dynamically Frozen regime, perpetual stability of the phases is expected.
