Figure~\ref{fig:susvsC} shows the variation of $\chi^{1}_\omega$, $\chi^{2}_\omega$, $\chi^{3}_\omega$, $\chi^{4}_\omega$,  $\chi^{5}_\omega$, and $\chi^{6}_\omega$ with temperature at zero baryochemical potential for different values of $\omega$. The solid lines are the estimation from the ideal HRG model, whereas the dashed lines are those from the VDWHRG model. We will use this notation throughout the study. In the present work, we examine four different values of rotation, such as $\omega$=0.01, 0.02, 0.03, 0.04 fm$^{-1}$ for our study. The considered values of $\omega$ in this study are close to following the recent experimental polarization measurement $\Lambda$ and $\bar{\Lambda}$ hyperons, $\omega$ $\simeq$ ($P_{\Lambda}$ + $P_{\bar{\Lambda}}$)$k_{B}$T/{$\hslash$} $\simeq$ (9 $\pm$ 1) $\times$ 10$^{21}$ sec$^{-1}$ $\simeq$ (0.02 - 0.04) fm$^{-1}$~\cite{STAR:2017ckg}. We find that the magnitude of rotational susceptibilities increases monotonically from first to sixth order of susceptibilities for all values of temperature and rotation. In addition, at a given temperature, a higher $\omega$ gives a higher susceptibility, implying that the rotation enhances the different orders of rotational susceptibilities or the so-called spin polarization.  A similar finding for first-order susceptibility is reported in the three-flavor NJL model~\cite{Sun:2021hxo}. A significant increase in the susceptibilities of fifth and/or sixth order (also called as hyperorders) is observed as a function of both temperature and rotation. The progressive enhancement of rotational susceptibilities in the hadron resonance gas model from first to sixth orders indicates that the hyperorders are more sensitive to the angular momentum fluctuations. Furthermore, considering the interactions among the hadrons, the rotational susceptibility value is suppressed compared to those in the non-interacting case. \\ 
