The first-order derivative of pressure (or free energy) with respect to rotation is called the rotation density or spin density, which is similar to the number density defined as the first-order derivative of pressure with respect to chemical potential. In general, the spin density contains crucial information about the system, such as the spin polarization of hadrons induced due to rotation (called rotational polarization). The angular momentum and the moment of inertia can also be calculated from the first-order derivative of free energy with respect to rotation. Therefore, studying the first-order and higher-order susceptibilities due to rotation and understanding the role of spin to describe the hot hadronic/QCD matter evolution is an important topic of research in relativistic heavy-ion collisions. With this motivation, we have explicitly plotted $\chi^{2}_{\omega}$ for spin-0, spin-1/2, spin-1, and spin-3/2 particles in a hadron gas to understand the effect of spin on the rotational susceptibility observable. The upper and lower panel of Fig.~\ref{fig:spin} shows the variation of $\chi^{2}_{\omega}$ as a function of temperature and $\omega$, respectively, for spin-0, spin-1/2, and spin-1, spin-3/2 hadrons for a rigidly rotating system. We observe that at lower temperatures ($\sim (80-130)$ MeV), $\chi^{2}_{\omega}$ is found to have negligible contribution from spin-1/2 and spin-1, spin-3/2 particles, while spin-0 particles have substantial contribution. However, at high temperatures, all the baryons start populating the hadronic matter and contribute to thermodynamic susceptibility significantly. At high temperature, the spin-0 particles (mainly dominated by pions ($\pi^{\pm}$, $\pi^{0}$)) have the maximum contribution to $\chi^{2} _{\omega}$, followed by spin-1 ($\rho^{\pm}$, $\rho^{0}$), spin-3/2 ($\Delta^{++}$, $\Delta^{+}$, $\Delta^{0}$, $\Delta^{-}$), and spin-1/2 [protons($p$), neutrons($n$)]. The lower panel of Fig.~\ref{fig:spin} shows the second-order rotational susceptibility $\chi^{2}_{\omega}$ obtained at T = 155 MeV, and increases as a function of rotational chemical potential for all kinds of particles. Moreover, a system of hadron gas with only spin-0 particles has more response to rotation and the corresponding susceptibility $\chi^{2}_{\omega}$, followed by  spin-1, spin-3/2, and spin-1/2 particles. The van der Waals interactions among the hadrons lower the $\chi^{2}_{\omega}$ as compared to the no interaction case.\\

