In literature, the susceptibility ratios are studied to describe various physical and critical phenomena in heavy-ion collisions. For example, considering analogy with susceptibility ratios defined for conserved charge (baryon number, electric charge, and strangeness) fluctuations and correlations, it is believed that $\chi^{2}_\omega$/$\chi^{1}_\omega$ is related to the diffusivity of angular momentum, whereas $\chi^{3}_\omega$/$\chi^{1}_\omega$ measures skewness and is sensitive to the proximity of criticality. The quantity $\chi^{4}_\omega$/$\chi^{2}_\omega$ measures kurtosis and peaks near the phase transition. It also helps to isolate the non-equilibrium effects. The ratios like $\chi^{6}_\omega$/$\chi^{2}_\omega$, and $\chi^{5}_\omega$/$\chi^{1}_\omega$ show sensitivity to critical behavior, etc. It is expected that in the ratios of susceptibility, the volume effect is cancelled out, and they could serve as ideal observables for studying the angular momentum fluctuations.
In principle, the susceptibility ratios associated with conserved charges can be experimentally measured by the corresponding cumulant ratios of the event-by-event conserved charge multiplicity distribution. As we mentioned in section~\ref{flucor}, although for this study it is not straightforward to assign a conserved charge associated with the rotational susceptibility, one can still infer about the angular momentum fluctuations and correlations through the system response function, so-called rotational susceptibility. Nevertheless, it is important to note that measuring the angular momentum fluctuations in ultra-relativistic heavy-ion collision experiments is a non-trivial task.  To investigate the impact of rotation on the aforementioned phenomena, we have systematically studied the susceptibility ratios in Fig.~\ref{fig:susratiosvsC}. Figure~\ref{fig:susratiosvsC} illustrates the variation of $\chi^{2}_\omega$/$\chi^{1}_\omega$, $\chi^{3}_\omega$/$\chi^{2}_\omega$, $\chi^{4}_\omega$/$\chi^{3}_\omega$,  $\chi^{3}_\omega$/$\chi^{1}_\omega$, $\chi^{5}_\omega$/$\chi^{3}_\omega$, $\chi^{5}_\omega$/$\chi^{1}_\omega$, $\chi^{4}_\omega$/$\chi^{2}_\omega$,  $\chi^{6}_\omega$/$\chi^{4}_\omega$, and $\chi^{6}_\omega$/$\chi^{2}_\omega$ ratios as a functions of temperature $T$ and rotation $\omega$ at zero baryon chemical potential. It is observed that all these ratios increase with an increase in $T$ and $\omega$ for both ideal HRG and VDWHRG models, although the slope of the curves varies as a function of $T$ and $\omega$. Interestingly, we found that the interactions among the hadrons play a crucial role in the rotational susceptibility ratios as functions of temperature. At intermediate temperature ranges ($\sim (120-160)$ MeV), the rotational susceptibilities ratios is higher for VDWHRG model, however, at higher temperatures ($\sim (160-180)$ MeV) and higher rotation ($\sim (0.03-0.04)$ fm$^{-1}$), the interaction suppresses the rotational susceptibilities ratios. Furthermore, an ordering in the even and odd orders of rotational susceptibilities ratios is observed such that, $\chi^{2}_\omega$/$\chi^{4}_\omega$ $>$  $\chi^{4}_\omega$/$\chi^{6}_\omega$ $>$  $\chi^{2}_\omega$/$\chi^{6}_\omega$ and $\chi^{3}_\omega$/$\chi^{1}_\omega$ $>$  $\chi^{5}_\omega$/$\chi^{3}_\omega$ $>$  $\chi^{5}_\omega$/$\chi^{1}_\omega$.

