The precession angle $\theta$ of the orbital plane with respect to the initial $x-y$ plane is calculated  as 
\begin{equation}
\label{alpha}
    \theta(t) = \text{arccos} \left( \frac{\vec{L}(t) \cdot \vec{L}(0)}{L(t) L(0)} \right),
\end{equation}
where $\vec{L}(t)$ is the orbital angular momentum at time $t$. 

The in-plane precession can be calculated by considering two consecutive apocenter passages of the test star, such that after a full radial period the angular shift is equal to 
\begin{equation}
    \label{phi}
    \delta \varphi_{xy} = \text{arctan} \left( \frac{\Delta y _{\text{apo}}}{\Delta x_{\text{apo}}} \right).
\end{equation}
This quantity corresponds exactly to the in-plane orbital precession only if the test star, starting its motion in the $x-y$ plane, would follow a planar orbit. Since in our simulations the orbit is non-planar, this quantity represents the angular precession of the orbit projected onto the $x-y$ plane, which is the initial orbital plane of the test star.

